        procedure lookup_property(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            property_name: String
        ) @ret_caller true returns ref[Property]? linked


























        ; reorder_named_components#: give a NAMED tuple component its meaning.
        ;
        ; ★★★ The grammar has carried the name since the beginning -- `Component`
        ; is `Operand+ [':' Operand+]` and `Modeler.handle_component#` puts it in
        ; `Model.Component.name` -- and the construction path IGNORED it, filling
        ; positionally. Measured before this landed: `Rec(gamma: 3, alpha: 1,
        ; beta: 2)` on `define Rec (alpha, beta, gamma)` answered `3 1 2` at rc 0
        ; with nothing said. Worse than a wrong value: `check_one_component_type#`
        ; RETURNS EARLY on a named component -- exactly because the name meant
        ; nothing -- so the labelled spelling also switched OFF the type check
        ; that would have caught it. A spelling that looks like documentation and
        ; silently disables a gate is the worst shape this compiler has.
        ;
        ; What it does: when ANY component carries a name, resolve every name to
        ; a property index and return the components in DECLARATION order, with
        ; the names STRIPPED so the positional check downstream applies to them
        ; like any other. No name anywhere -> the input unchanged, so every
        ; existing construction in the tree emits byte-identically.
        ;
        ; ★ The rules are deliberately strict, because each looser one has a
        ; silent failure behind it:
        ;   * name one field and you must name them ALL -- a mixed list would
        ;     make the reader compute which positions the labels shifted.
        ;   * an unknown name is an error, never a positional fallback.
        ;   * a field named twice is an error; the last-wins rule would make the
        ;     first value's side effects invisible.
        ;   * the named fields must be a PREFIX of the declaration. A trailing
        ;     omission is what fill_default_components# fills; a MIDDLE one it
        ;     cannot, and inventing a value here would be the uninitialised-field
        ;     class this path already exists to prevent.
        ; ★ `rp` is DECLARED, not inferred. The body constructs nothing itself
        ; -- it only forwards to place_named_components# -- so page-inference
        ; rule 1 (`body_may_allocate`) correctly answers NO, and the vector the
        ; callee builds then lands on THIS routine's frame, which is released
        ; at the return. Measured: one named construction compiled, TWO in one
        ; file segfaulted the planner (rc 139), because the second reused the
        ; released frame. The mangled name is where it shows: the sibling
        ; restamp_components# carries `PN4scaly6memory4PageE` and this one did
        ; not.
        procedure reorder_named_components(
            rp,
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            loc: Span,
            provided: ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]?,
            ctor_type: PlannedType
        ) @ret_param_3 true returns ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]? linked



























        ; The Structure half of reorder_named_components#, split out so the
        ; caller stays one statement per rule (the s178 alloca-pool discipline).
        procedure place_named_components(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            s: Structure,
            loc: Span,
            pv: Vector[PlannedComponent],
            named: size_t
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]? linked
































































































































        ; fill_default_components: for a data-class construction that matched
        ; no explicit init#, append default-value components for every
        ; property beyond the provided count (`Config()` with all-defaulted
        ; fields, or `Config(true)` filling the trailing default). Mirrors
        ; stage-0 Planner.cpp:9229-9275. Returns a fresh component vector when
        ; defaults were appended, else the input unchanged. Only fires when
        ; the construction genuinely omitted trailing properties that ALL have
        ; defaults; a full construction and a concept whose missing properties
        ; lack defaults are both no-ops (keeps compiler-own emission unchanged
        ; where every construction provides all args → fixpoint-safe).
        procedure fill_default_components(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable struct_name: String,
            loc: Span,
            provided: ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]?,
            ctor_type: PlannedType,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType]
        ) @page true @direct true returns ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]? io linked




























































































































































































































































































        ; find_initializer: locate an Initializer on a Struct-shaped
        ; concept whose parameter types are pairwise compatible with
        ; `arg_types`. Returns null when no match. Deliberately linear —
        ; matching against the first compatible overload.
        ; check_initializer_match: returns the Initializer pointer if
        ; its input matches arg_types (after skipping any implicit
        ; page parameter, which is already excluded from input by the
        ; Modeler). `exact_only` selects types_equal vs types_compatible.
        procedure check_initializer_match(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable ini: Initializer,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            exact_only: bool
        ) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool io linked







































        ; (The former concept_is_heap_backed NAME ALLOWLIST lived here. It is
        ; gone: residency is now DERIVED from the concept's own bodies by
        ; concept_needs_this_residency below — see its comment. Vector and
        ; String still classify as stack-safe values by construction: their
        ; bodies contain no `^this` and no `Page.get(this)`.)

        ; concept_needs_this_residency: true iff the named concept derives a
        ; page from its own address anywhere in its bodies — a `^this`
        ; construction (Type or Matrix life) or a `Page.get(this ...)` call,
        ; in any init, method, operator, or the deinit. Such an instance MUST
        ; live on a page; a stack instance would compute a garbage page
        ; header. SOURCE-DRIVEN: this replaced the concept_is_heap_backed
        ; name allowlist (Array/List/builders/HashSet/HashMap/StringBuilder
        ; all classify via their own bodies — Array/builders through the
        ; two-step `let own_page Page.get(this as pointer[void])`, List
        ; through `Node[T]^this` in add, HashSet/HashMap/StringBuilder
        ; through generic `^this` inits). Consulted on the fall-through
        ; construction paths (Unspecified life) in collapse_operand_sequence;
        ; explicit #/^ constructions never reach it.
        ; ================================================================
        ; --interface-facts (tools/interface/geninterface.py): per routine of
        ; the ROOT package what a bodyless declaration must say that the
        ; planner derives from its body -- the caller page (R1..R7, part of
        ; the mangled name) and whether it stores an argument beyond its frame
        ; -- and per concept whether a construction of it must be page-resident.
        ; One line per fact, keyed by file and source offset; the generator
        ; does the text surgery.
        ; ================================================================

        procedure interface_facts(mutable this: Planner, m: Module) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns String io linked


        procedure facts_module(mutable this: Planner, m: Module, generic: bool) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns String io linked

















        function facts_line(tag: String, file: String, span: Span, kind: String, name: String, generic: bool, page: bool, explicit: bool, persists: bool) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns String linked

























        procedure facts_members(mutable this: Planner, file: String, members: Vector[Member], generic: bool) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns String io linked































        procedure facts_function(mutable this: Planner, file: String, f: Function, generic: bool) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns String io linked































































        procedure facts_concept(mutable this: Planner, file: String, c: Concept, generic: bool) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns String io linked






















































































        procedure concept_needs_this_residency(mutable this: Planner, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked






















        function structure_members_have_this_alloc(this: Planner, s: Structure) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked































        function structure_init_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, s: Structure) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked
















        function impl_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, impl: Implementation) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked










        function action_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, a: Action) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked






        function operands_have_this_alloc(this: Planner, ops: Vector[Operand]) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked


































        function operand_is_page_get(op: Operand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked


































        ; True when the operand is the argument shape of a `this`-receiving
        ; call: a Tuple whose first component's value leads with `this`
        ; (`Page.get(this as pointer[void])`), or a bare `this` (parenless).
        function operand_leads_with_this(op: Operand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked






























        function operand_is_bare_this(op: Operand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked










        function type_is_bare_this(ty: Type) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked










        ; Exhaustive Expression walker, mirroring operand_references_rp /
        ; operand_has_call_life below: a `^this` construction or a
        ; `Page.get(this)` call nested inside a control-flow operand
        ; (if/choose/match/for/while/repeat/try/lambda) must be visible, else
        ; the concept silently classifies stack-safe and bare constructions
        ; stack-alloc an instance whose Page.get(this) then reads a garbage
        ; page header.
        function operand_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, op: Operand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked




















































































































        function tuple_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, tp: Tuple) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked
















        function block_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, bk: Block) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked
















        function matrix_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, mx: Matrix) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked
























        function statement_has_this_alloc(this: Planner, st: Statement) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked






















        function type_life_is_this(t: Type) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked


        function lifetime_is_this(lt: Lifetime) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked













        procedure find_initializer(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable struct_name: String,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[Initializer]? io linked




















































































        ; get_property_index: position of a named property in the struct.
        ; Returns -1 if the type isn't a struct or the property is missing.
        procedure get_property_index(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            property_name: String
        ) @ret_param_0 true returns int linked


























        ; variant_payload_points_at: does a POINTER-SHAPED variant payload
        ; point at `variant_name`? A payload declared `ref[X]` / `pointer[X]`
        ; carries the WRAPPER in its own `name`, so the `when v: X` spelling —
        ; the one every by-value arm uses — matched nothing, lookup_variant_tag
        ; answered -1 and plan_when left the tag at 0. Every such arm then
        ; dispatched to variant 0 in silence: measured on a two-arm union over
        ; ref payloads, `when a: RecA` took arm 0 and `when b: RecB` fell
        ; through to the else, rc 0, no diagnostic. The B3 guard in plan_when
        ; cannot see it either — it exempts an arm name that is itself a
        ; concept, and X always is.
        ; ★The two arms of this shape already in the tree (dazzle's
        ; Insn.MakeFlowObj, opensp's MessageArg.Vec) are unaffected: both are
        ; written with the arm's FIELD name, which always resolved.
        ; ★Asked only AFTER a full pass with the old rules has found nothing,
        ; so every `when` that resolves today resolves to the SAME arm and
        ; emission does not move.
        ; ★`Option` is deliberately NOT unwrapped: an optional payload is a
        ; different value space from the value it may hold, and `when v: X`
        ; over `V: X?` would bind something that can be absent.
        function variant_payload_points_at(
            v_type: ref[scalyc.compiler.Model.Type],
            variant_name: String
        ) @ret_caller true returns bool linked




























        ; lookup_variant: find a union variant by name and return its tag.
        ; Returns -1 if the concept is missing or not a Union, or if the
        ; variant name is not present.
        procedure lookup_variant_tag(
            mutable this: Planner,
            union_name: String,
            variant_name: String
        ) @ret_param_0 true returns int linked




























































        ; lookup_variant_tag_by_name: lookup_variant_tag restricted to the
        ; variant's own FIELD name, never its payload TYPE name.
        ;
        ; The type-name arm above exists for `choose … when TypeName: x`, which
        ; matches by the payload type. Reading a CONSTRUCTION that way is dead
        ; code in every well-formed program — if the payload record is visible,
        ; lookup_concept has already resolved the name as a struct ctor — and it
        ; was live in exactly one broken one: a record the file cannot SEE.
        ; `U.First(Rec(11, 22, 33))` with `Rec` hidden made lookup_concept answer
        ; null, so the s185 rescue for a bare `Some(5)` ran, the payload-type arm
        ; answered on "Rec", and the rescue returned a variant construction of
        ; the OUTER union — same tag, only the first argument as its payload,
        ; rc 0 and not a word (2026-08-16: 34 SIGSEGVs in Array::get_length,
        ; three subsystems from the cause). The rescue standing in FRONT of the
        ; not-found machinery is what swallowed the `use` hint that
        ; xfail_use_vis_hidden.scaly proves is otherwise given.
        ;
        ; So the rescue asks this narrower question and an invisible record falls
        ; through into the existing hard diagnostic — no new message needed.
        ; lookup_variant_tag# itself is unchanged, so `choose` keeps matching by
        ; the payload type as it must; and the three tests that
        ; cover the rescue (definitions__option-some, result-ok, result-err) all
        ; match on the FIELD name and are the refutation control.
        procedure lookup_variant_tag_by_name(
            mutable this: Planner,
            union_name: String,
            variant_name: String
        ) @ret_param_0 true returns int linked
























        ; lookup_variant_type: fetch the payload PlannedType for a named
        ; variant. Returns null when the concept isn't a union, the
        ; variant doesn't exist, or the variant has no payload (tag-only
        ; variants like Option.None).
        procedure lookup_variant_type(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable union_name: String,
            variant_name: String
        ) returns ref[PlannedType]? io linked








































































        ; match_type_pattern: walk `pattern` and `actual` in parallel,
        ; collecting generic-parameter → concrete-type bindings.
        ; Recursion handles nested generics (e.g. matching `Vector[T]`
        ; against `Vector[int]` yields `T -> int`).
        ;
        ; Runs purely — returns a fresh Vector each call rather than
        ; mutating a caller-supplied accumulator, because passing a
        ; mutable Array as a parameter doesn't propagate in Scaly's
        ; value-semantics model.
        function match_type_pattern(
            this: Planner,
            pattern: PlannedType,
            actual: PlannedType
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns Vector[KeyValuePair[String, PlannedType]] linked



























































        ; infer_type_arguments: given a function's parameter types
        ; (possibly containing generic parameters like T, K) and the
        ; actual argument types at a call site, infer the concrete type
        ; assignments. Returns a flat Vector of name→type pairs; may
        ; contain duplicates for a generic parameter inferred from
        ; multiple positions (all should agree — agreement is checked
        ; downstream during substitution).
        function infer_type_arguments(
            this: Planner,
            param_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns Vector[KeyValuePair[String, PlannedType]] linked

































        ; instantiate_generic_function: monomorphise a generic function
        ; (one whose parameter types reference type-parameter names like
        ; T / K / V) against a substitution mapping those names to
        ; concrete types. The resulting PlannedFunction has each use of
        ; T replaced with its concrete binding, and is recorded in the
        ; instantiation cache via plan_function.
        procedure instantiate_generic_function(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            args: Vector[KeyValuePair[String, PlannedType]]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedFunction io linked

















        ; ================================================================
        ; instantiate_generic: monomorphise a generic Concept against a
        ; concrete type-argument list. Binds each GenericParameter name
        ; to its corresponding argument in `type_substitutions`, plans
        ; the concept's definition so the struct-layout cache gets the
        ; instantiated version, then restores the substitution stack.
        ;
        ; Returns the resulting PlannedType with `generics` populated
        ; and `mangled_name` set via mangle_structure. Missing concepts
        ; fall back to a fresh type variable — keeps the planner
        ; productive on incomplete programs.
        ; ================================================================
        procedure instantiate_generic(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable concept_name: String,
            mutable args: Vector[PlannedType],
            loc: Span
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedType io linked


























































































































































































        ; ================================================================
        ; check_function_match: test a single Function candidate against
        ; argument types. Returns a ref to the return type if it matches,
        ; null otherwise. `exact_only` selects types_equal (true) vs
        ; types_compatible (false) for parameter/argument comparison.
        ; 'this' parameters are skipped implicitly.
        ; ================================================================
        ; Pure match predicate for overload resolution passes 1 (exact) and
        ; 2 (compatible). NON-`#` on purpose: a #-subcall like plan_type#
        ; allocates on THIS function's local page (not the caller's rp), so
        ; the candidate parameter types planned only to compare against the
        ; args are reclaimed when this returns — instead of accumulating on
        ; the persistent region for every losing candidate of every call
        ; (the dominant transient in -c, measured ~1.2-1.9 MB/LOC of planner
        ; allocation). resolve_function_call# rebuilds the WINNER on rp via
        ; check_function_match#; only the winner's types persist. Mirrors
        ; check_function_match#'s arity + per-param comparison exactly so the
        ; rebuild always agrees. plan_type# is side-effect-free (no
        ; instantiation — pass 3 keeps using check_function_match# directly).
        ; candidate_matches_string_i8: a String argument prefers a
        ; pointer[i8] parameter — the pre-feature representation of a string
        ; literal (before string literals became real String values). Matches
        ; only when EVERY argument is either exactly the parameter type OR a
        ; String against a pointer[i8] parameter, AND at least one String→i8
        ; substitution occurs. Used as a resolution tie-break between the
        ; exact pass (which still picks a real String-parameter overload like
        ; `append(String)`) and the general compatible pass (which would let a
        ; namespace member like Console.print(pointer[const_char]) win for a
        ; bare `print("x")` and then free-mangle to a phantom symbol).
        procedure candidate_matches_string_i8(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType]
        ) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool io linked
















































        ; type_is_i8_pointer: pointer[i8] specifically (the canonical string
        ; literal target). Distinct from type_is_cstr_pointer, which also
        ; accepts const_char/char/u8.
        function type_is_i8_pointer(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked

















        procedure candidate_matches(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            exact_only: bool
        ) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool io linked















































        ; candidate_matches_char_fold: pure predicate for the char-fold
        ; fallback pass. Arity + per-param, where a param that fails the
        ; compatible check still matches when it is char/u8 and the argument
        ; is a foldable String constant. Side-effect-free like candidate_matches
        ; (plan_type# only) — the winner is rebuilt by check_function_match#
        ; with char_fold=true, which folds the operand at the call site.
        procedure candidate_matches_char_fold(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]
        ) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool io linked











































































        ; Phase-5 class 1: a persist into a receiver that is itself a
        ; frame-local, non-escaping binding does not outlive the frame —
        ; receiver and argument share the frame's lifetime, so promoting
        ; the argument (or hard-erroring on a missing rp) would be a false
        ; positive. The Planner-instance test code hits exactly this shape
        ; (a local Planner collecting constraints built from local-page
        ; types). `this`, parameters, and escaping/promoted locals all
        ; outlive the frame -> not frame-local.
        function persist_receiver_is_frame_local(this: Planner, is_method: bool, recv: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked




























        ; Phase-5 class 1: memoized persist summary. Key = the callee's
        ; mangled name; value = function_persists_param_guts over the model
        ; body, computed once. Only consulted when a call argument carries
        ; Local life (see the resolution sites), so the walk runs rarely.
        procedure persists_args_for(mutable this: Planner, mangled: String, f: Function) @ret_param_0 true returns bool linked


































        ; Phase-5 class 1 gate: does any argument type carry Local life —
        ; the Design-B local-flip stamp on a call result whose guts live on
        ; THIS frame's local page? Only such calls can need the persist
        ; promotion; sigiled (`#`) arguments carry Call life, so the gate
        ; keeps the summary walk off every sigiled call site (neutrality).
        ; the deep gate's mask for f (which input positions persist), all-ones
        ; when the gate is off or the function is not in the pre-pass
        function persists_mask_for(this: Planner, f: Function) @ret_param_0 true returns u64 linked


















        ; does an argument at a PERSISTING position carry Local life
        function args_have_local_life_masked(arg_types: Vector[PlannedType], mask: u64, off: size_t) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked






















        function args_have_local_life(arg_types: Vector[PlannedType]) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked













        procedure check_function_match(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            exact_only: bool,
            char_fold: bool,
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[MethodMatch]? io linked
















































































































































































































































        ; s-LSP: is `prefix` a namespace declaring a member function named
        ; `suffix` whose first parameter is `this`? Used by the dotted-call
        ; fallback to recognise a namespace this-method invoked qualified
        ; with an explicit receiver (`NS.f(recv, ...)`). Mirrors the member
        ; scan in stage-0's name-only Namespace path (Planner.cpp:8663-8674).
        ; Kept out of collapse_operand_sequence# (its frame is the heaviest
        ; in the compiler) so stage-1 emits it correctly into stage-2.
        function is_namespace_this_method(this: Planner, prefix: String, suffix: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked















































        ; s-LSP: resolve `NS.f(recv, ...)` where `f(this: T, ...)` is a
        ; namespace this-method invoked qualified with an explicit receiver.
        ; Returns the MethodMatch (this-implicit, so `_ZN<ns><fn>E…`) or null.
        ; Builds the receiver-dropped argument list in ITS OWN frame and
        ; resolves the this-implicit overload; the caller's namespace-mangling
        ; block then rebuilds the namespaced mangled name. Kept whole here so
        ; collapse_operand_sequence# adds no locals (its s178 alloca pool
        ; mis-aliases any added there — see the call site).
        procedure resolve_namespace_this_method(
            mutable this: Planner,
            prefix: String,
            mutable suffix: String,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[MethodMatch]? io linked
































        ; concept_has_static: does `prefix` name a Structure concept declaring a
        ; this-LESS (static) member function called `suffix`? A qualified call
        ; `Type.method(...)` on such a concept is a static call that MUST carry
        ; the concept parent in its mangled name — the s155 concept-static path
        ; (gated match_ref=null in collapse_operand_sequence#) does exactly that.
        ; resolve_dotted_call# defers to it (returns null) for these, so the
        ; bare-suffix resolve below can never mis-match an unrelated same-named
        ; free function. This is load-bearing for opensp: every module exposes a
        ; `test()` static and the function-only ones ALSO register `test` in the
        ; free registry (register_concept_recursive's Namespace arm), so a bare
        ; `test` resolve returns SOME `test` and `StringC.test()` mis-mangled to
        ; the enclosing/sibling `test` — the aggregator self-recursed. Gated to
        ; Structures only: Namespace-member calls (Console.println) keep their
        ; existing bare-then-namespace resolution untouched.
        procedure concept_has_static(mutable this: Planner, prefix: String, suffix: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked




































































        ; s-LSP: the dotted-call fallback's resolution, as ONE call so the
        ; caller (collapse_operand_sequence#) keeps a single `set match_ref:`
        ; statement — adding a branch there perturbed its s178 alloca pool.
        ; Try the normal this-implicit overload first; if that misses, try the
        ; namespace this-method-with-explicit-receiver form.
        ; ★★★The OPERANDS travel with the types since 2026-09-06. Both resolves
        ; below used to hand resolve_function_call# an EMPTY operand vector, and
        ; every literal fallback of the matcher (the char fold, the float retype,
        ; the string-literal-into-Slice[char] materialisation) asks the OPERAND
        ; whether it is a constant -- with no operands in hand the fallback pass
        ; never matched, and a NAMESPACE function taking a `Slice[char]` could
        ; not be reached by a literal at all: `Planner.make_primitive_type("void",
        ; "v")` was a hard "function not found" while the identical call on a
        ; Structure's static resolved (that path selects by name and arity and
        ; folds at the construction site). Found converting the compiler's own
        ; ASCII-literal parameters; gate `tests/regress/namespace_literal_slice_arg.scaly`.
        procedure resolve_dotted_call(
            mutable this: Planner,
            prefix: String,
            mutable suffix: String,
            mutable arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]
        ) @page true @returnpage true @ret_caller true returns ref[MethodMatch]? io linked













        ; resolve_static_method_on: resolve an UNQUALIFIED bare call `fn_name`
        ; against the this-less (static) member functions of a named Structure
        ; concept — the enclosing-struct sibling-static case. A Structure's
        ; statics are not in the free-function registry (they aren't reachable
        ; via resolve_function_call#) and, having no `this`, cannot go through
        ; the s101 implicit-this dispatch, so `bit(m)` inside ModeInfo's static
        ; `build_table()` would otherwise report "function not found". This is
        ; the exact resolution the s155 qualified-static path (`Type.method`)
        ; performs, factored into a helper so the collapse_operand_sequence#
        ; call site stays a single `set match_ref:` statement (adding an inline
        ; branch there perturbs its s178 alloca pool — see resolve_dotted_call).
        ; Returns null when the struct is unknown or declares no matching
        ; static; the caller then falls through to its remaining fallbacks.
        ; Overload note: prefer a static whose arity matches the argument count;
        ; otherwise take the first same-named static (mirrors s155's name-only
        ; pick for the single-overload common case).
        procedure resolve_static_method_on(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            fn_name: String,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand],
            loc: Span
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[MethodMatch]? io linked






















































































































































































        ; ================================================================
        ; resolve_function_call: return a MethodMatch describing the
        ; chosen overload of `name` for the given argument types. Two-pass
        ; overload resolution: exact matches first, then compatible
        ; (widened), then generic instantiation. 'this' parameters are
        ; implicitly skipped.
        ;
        ; Returns null when no overload matches; callers fall back to a
        ; fresh type variable so the constraint solver can recover.
        ; ================================================================
        ; Bind a generic function's parameters to an EXPLICIT type-argument
        ; list and monomorphise it. Mirrors the inferred path (the
        ; instantiate_generic_function call in lookup_method#) in every respect
        ; but where the bindings come from.
        procedure instantiate_explicit_generic_call(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            generics: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[MethodMatch]? io linked

































































        procedure resolve_function_call(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable name: String,
            mutable arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]
        ) @page true @returnpage true @ret_caller true returns ref[MethodMatch]? io linked


        ; ★★★A generic FREE function whose type parameter appears only in the
        ; RETURN type has nothing to infer from, and until 2026-09-04 this walk
        ; simply matched the UNINSTANTIATED declaration: the emitted symbol
        ; carried no type argument, its return type was the generic `Slice[T]`,
        ; and `sizeof T` folded to 8. The caller read a garbage value at rc 0.
        ; The explicit list (`f[u64](...)`) rides operand 0 as a PlannedType's
        ; `generics` — measured — so it is threaded down here and bound by
        ; POSITION, exactly as instantiate_generic_function binds an inferred
        ; one. Inference still runs first for everything it can reach.
        procedure resolve_function_call_generic(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable name: String,
            mutable arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand],
            explicit_generics: ref[Vector[PlannedType]]?
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[MethodMatch]? io linked














































































































































































































        ; mangle_binary_operator: convenience wrapper that constructs
        ; the PlannedItem list mangle_operator expects from two
        ; PlannedType arguments. Holder Vectors keep both types alive
        ; on rp so their refs are stable for the Item construction.
        procedure mangle_binary_operator(
            mutable this: Planner,
            op_name: String,
            left: PlannedType,
            right: PlannedType
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns String linked












        ; mangle_unary_operator: same idea for prefix/unary operators.
        procedure mangle_unary_operator(
            mutable this: Planner,
            op_name: String,
            operand: PlannedType
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns String linked









        ; make_planned_call: assemble a PlannedCall for a resolved call
        ; site. Callers provide the mangled symbol, result type, and
        ; planned argument list; flags default to the common
        ; "non-intrinsic, non-operator, non-throwing, no-page" case.
        procedure make_planned_call(
            mutable this: Planner,
            loc: Span,
            name: String,
            mangled_name: String,
            mutable result_type: PlannedType,
            mutable args: Vector[PlannedOperand],
            requires_page: bool
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedCall io linked





































        ; True when t is pointer[const_char] / pointer[i8] / pointer[char] /
        ; pointer[u8] — a C-string parameter position. A String argument bound
        ; to such a parameter must be lowered to its c_data content pointer.
        function type_is_cstr_pointer(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked


























        ; coerce_string_args_to_cstr: for a resolved call, wrap each String
        ; argument whose matched parameter is a pointer[const_char]-like type
        ; in a PlannedAs(target = param_type). emit_as then lowers the String
        ; to its NUL-terminated c_data pointer (a constant to the plain .str
        ; global; a variable via get_buffer). This decision MUST live in the
        ; planner: at the LLVM level a by-value String parameter and a
        ; const_char parameter are both `ptr`, and a generic instantiated
        ; method's parameter is not present in current_plan.functions — so the
        ; emitter cannot tell them apart. Here the substituted parameter type
        ; is known. param_types excludes `this` in every resolution path, so
        ; the user arguments are tail-aligned (a prepended receiver, present
        ; for an instance-method dispatch, is absorbed by the offset).
        procedure coerce_string_args_to_cstr(
            mutable this: Planner,
            args: Vector[PlannedOperand],
            param_types: Vector[PlannedType]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns Vector[PlannedOperand] linked











































        ; field_is_cstr_pointer: type_is_cstr_pointer# for a FIELD, which may
        ; still name the concept's own generic parameter. `Slice[T]` declares
        ; `data: pointer[T]`, so the unsubstituted field type answers no for
        ; every instantiation -- and Slice is exactly the concept that runs the
        ; tuple path for all of its constructions. Resolve the inner name
        ; against the construction's generic ARGUMENTS by position, the same
        ; correspondence the instantiation itself uses. Only the one shape is
        ; resolved: a deeper nesting is not a cstr pointer either way.
        function field_is_cstr_pointer(
            ft: PlannedType,
            params: Vector[GenericParameter],
            ctor_type: PlannedType
        ) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked













































        ; subst_cstr_pointer: the target type for the PlannedAs wrap. Where the
        ; field type is already concrete it IS the target; where it named the
        ; concept's generic parameter (`Slice[T].data`) there is no
        ; substituted PlannedType to hand, and make_cstring_type# is the honest
        ; one -- field_is_cstr_pointer# has already established that the
        ; instantiated element is a C character type, and at LLVM level every
        ; arm of that family is `ptr`.
        function subst_cstr_pointer(
            ft: PlannedType,
            params: Vector[GenericParameter],
            ctor_type: PlannedType
        ) @page true @direct true returns PlannedType linked






        ; coerce_string_components_to_cstr: the TUPLE-path counterpart of
        ; coerce_string_args_to_cstr#, closing the same hole one level down. A
        ; construction that matched no init# falls through to the positional-
        ; tuple path, where NO parameter list steers the argument -- so a
        ; String literal bound to a pointer[const_char]-like FIELD was stored
        ; as the varint-prefixed String constant (`@.sconst = c"\02as\00"`) and
        ; the field then pointed at the LENGTH BYTE. `Holder("as", 2)` read 2
        ; where the same record with a matching init# read 97; rc 0, nothing
        ; said, and the two constants sit one line apart in the IR
        ; (`.sconst = [4 x i8] c"\02as\00"` against `.str = [3 x i8] c"as\00"`).
        ;
        ; The FIELD type is the only thing that can decide this, exactly as the
        ; PARAMETER type is at a call: at LLVM level a by-value String and a
        ; const_char pointer are both `ptr`.
        ;
        ; Alignment is POSITIONAL, the tuple path's own rule. A NAMED component
        ; is skipped rather than guessed at, and so is anything but a lone
        ; String operand -- under-coercion is today's behaviour, over-coercion
        ; would be a new wrong pointer. Emission-neutral where nothing coerces:
        ; the vector is rebuilt only when a component actually changed.
        procedure coerce_string_components_to_cstr(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable struct_name: String,
            comps: ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]?,
            ctor_type: PlannedType
        ) @page true @direct true returns ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]? io linked














































































        ; report_component_type_mismatch#: the TUPLE path's type check, the
        ; counterpart of check_initializer_match# one level down.
        ;
        ; A construction that matched no init# is filled POSITIONALLY, and
        ; nothing ever compared component i against property i: `Holder(
        ; buf.get_buffer(), 7)` on `(n: size_t, p: pointer[char])` stored the
        ; pointer in the size_t field and the 7 in the pointer field, rc 0, and
        ; ran. Only the COUNT was checked -- too many arguments is `wrong
        ; number of arguments`, too few without defaults is
        ; fill_default_components#, and everything between them was accepted.
        ;
        ; Runs AFTER coerce_string_components_to_cstr# so a String literal
        ; already carries the field's cstr type: checking before it would
        ; report every `Holder("as", 2)` that coercion exists to make right.
        ;
        ; The acceptor is type_conformance_accepted#, the LOOSE one the four
        ; declared-type positions use. types_compatible# alone reports the
        ; region-construction and Option-into-pointer shapes that are the
        ; language rather than defects -- its own comment says so.
        procedure report_component_type_mismatch(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable struct_name: String,
            loc: Span,
            mutable comps: ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]?,
            ctor_type: PlannedType
        ) io linked






















        ; The walk half, split out so the entry above stays one statement per
        ; branch.
        ;
        ; ★The field types are planned inside enter_concept_generic_scope#, and
        ; a hand-rolled apply_name_substitution# over (parameters x generics)
        ; is NOT the same thing -- it was measured wrong: plan_type# resolves a
        ; field annotation against the AMBIENT substitutions, i.e. the ones the
        ; CALL SITE is being planned under. `ListIterator[Slot[T]](
        ; slot_ptr.head)` inside HashSet[String] planned its own `current:
        ; pointer[Node[T]]` as `pointer[Node[String]]` -- the ENCLOSING T --
        ; and reported the correct argument as a mismatch. The scope helper
        ; shadows exactly the names the concept declares itself, carries the
        ; occurs check, and is what every other consumer of a concept's own
        ; field types already uses.
        procedure check_component_types(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            mutable s: Structure,
            params: Vector[GenericParameter],
            mutable comps: ref[Vector[PlannedComponent]]?,
            ctor_type: PlannedType,
            loc: Span
        ) io linked



















        procedure check_components_in_scope(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            mutable s: Structure,
            mutable cv: Vector[PlannedComponent],
            loc: Span
        ) io linked










        ; One component against one property. Every early return here is a
        ; position the check deliberately says NOTHING about, and each is a
        ; measured exemption rather than a convenience.
        procedure check_one_component_type(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            mutable s: Structure,
            ci: size_t,
            mutable cv: Vector[PlannedComponent],
            loc: Span
        ) io linked


























































































































        ; The Option half's message, worded like report_option_arg# because it
        ; is the same finding one position over: the unwrap is named, since
        ; there is no flow narrowing of the TYPE and the cast is where the
        ; exit-21 trap is emitted.
        procedure report_option_component(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            field_name: String,
            field_type: PlannedType,
            arg_type: PlannedType,
            loc: Span
        ) @ret_nonlocal true linked





















        ; The message half, split out for the reason report_null_arg# splits
        ; its own: the walk above stays one statement per branch.
        procedure report_component_mismatch(
            mutable this: Planner,
            struct_name: String,
            field_name: String,
            field_type: PlannedType,
            arg_type: PlannedType,
            loc: Span
        ) @ret_nonlocal true linked



















        ; fold_string_args_to_char: for a resolved call, fold each bare
        ; double-quoted char literal argument (a chainless String constant)
        ; whose matched parameter is char/u8/i8 into a validated
        ; CharacterConstant. The counterpart to coerce_string_args_to_cstr for
        ; the scalar-char case: without this the String pointer would be passed
        ; into the char slot (silent wrong code). A multi-byte string here
        ; raises the hard "not a single character" diagnostic inside
        ; fold_string_operand_to_char. Same tail-alignment as
        ; coerce_string_args_to_cstr (param_types excludes `this`).
        procedure fold_string_args_to_char(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable args: Vector[PlannedOperand],
            mutable param_types: Vector[PlannedType]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns Vector[PlannedOperand] linked
























































































































        ; If `op` is a Phase-1 lambda binding name used as a callable head (a
        ; bare PlannedType with no generics, or an unresolved Variable, with no
        ; member-access chain), return that lambda name; otherwise the empty
        ; string. The lambda binding emits no runtime value, so the name arrives
        ; here unresolved — this is how a lambda call is recognized.
        function lambda_head_name(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @page true @direct true @ret_nonlocal true returns String linked





















        ; Resolve a Phase-1 lambda call `name(args)` into a direct call to the
        ; monomorphized function. `head_loc` is the call-site span, `lc_name`
        ; the lambda binding name, `lc_lam` its recorded model, `lc_tp` the
        ; argument Tuple. monomorphize_lambda# plans the body with the call's
        ; argument types and synthesizes a concrete top-level function; the
        ; captures it discovers are looked up at the call site (where they are
        ; in scope) and prepended as leading arguments (capture by value).
        procedure resolve_lambda_call(mutable this: Planner, head_loc: Span, lc_name: String, mutable lc_lam: Lambda, mutable lc_tp: PlannedTuple) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedOperand io linked















































































        ; collapse_operand_sequence: fold a flat operand list of the
        ; shape [lhs, op, rhs, op, rhs, ...] into a single PlannedOperand
        ; representing the combined expression. LEFT-ASSOCIATIVE — this
        ; drops operator precedence; a full precedence-climbing version
        ; is a later refactor. Empty and single-element inputs pass
        ; through unchanged.
        procedure collapse_operand_sequence(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]
        ) @page true @direct true returns PlannedOperand io linked

















































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































        ; check_operator_match: test a single Operator candidate against
        ; argument types. Same shape as check_function_match.
        procedure check_operator_match(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable o: Operator,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            exact_only: bool,
            char_fold: bool,
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]
        ) returns ref[PlannedType]? io linked



















































































        ; operator_planned_param_types: the operator's parameter types (skipping
        ; a leading `this`), planned to PlannedType. Used by resolve_operator_
        ; call#'s fold pass to hand the construction site the char/float types it
        ; must fold the literal operands to.
        procedure operator_planned_param_types(mutable this: Planner, mutable o: Operator) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns Vector[PlannedType] io linked

































        ; resolve_prefix_operator_call: for unary prefix operators like
        ; `-x` or `!flag`. Two-pass: exact matches first, then compatible.
        ; True iff an operator's implementation is Intrinsic (built-in
        ; primitive op, emitted inline by emit_intrinsic_*_op). A user-defined
        ; operator carries an Action body and must be emitted/called as a real
        ; function. Mirrors stage-0's holds_alternative<IntrinsicImpl>(Op->Impl).
        function operator_is_intrinsic(o: Operator) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked










        procedure resolve_prefix_operator_call(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable name: String,
            operand: PlannedType
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[PlannedType]? io linked




















































        ; resolve_operator_call: two-pass overload resolution for binary
        ; operators keyed on the operator symbol registry.
        procedure resolve_operator_call(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable name: String,
            left: PlannedType,
            right: PlannedType,
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]
        ) @page true @direct true returns ref[PlannedType]? io linked































































































































































































        ; mangle_method: build `_ZN<parent>methodEparam*` without the
        ; PlannedItem ceremony that mangle_function requires. Just takes
        ; a parent PlannedType and a Vector of param PlannedTypes.
        function mangle_method(
            this: Planner,
            method_name: String,
            parent: PlannedType,
            params: Vector[PlannedType],
            has_page_param: bool
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns String linked






































        ; method_params_match: does method `f`'s declared parameter list
        ; (after skipping a leading `this`) match `arg_types`? Mirrors the
        ; per-arg comparison in check_function_match#, but returns only a
        ; bool so lookup_method# can pick the right overload among several
        ; same-named methods. `exact_only` selects types_equal (pass 1) vs
        ; types_compatible (pass 2).
        ; widening_method_candidate#: between the EXACT pass and the first-
        ; COMPATIBLE pass of lookup_method#, prefer an overload that takes every
        ; integer argument WITHOUT narrowing it — first one of the argument's own
        ; signedness (`same_signedness`), then any. Rule 2 makes every integer
        ; compatible with every other, so the first-compatible pass took the
        ; first DECLARED overload: `sb.append(n.pos)` with an i32 picked
        ; `append(character: char)` and appended one garbage byte, rc 0
        ; (measured 2026-09-17). Every other argument must match exactly.
        procedure widening_method_candidate(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable candidates: Vector[Function],
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            same_signedness: bool
        ) @ret_param_1 true returns ref[Function]? io linked















        procedure method_params_widen(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            same_signedness: bool
        ) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool io linked














































        procedure method_params_match(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            exact_only: bool,
            char_fold: bool,
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]
        ) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool io linked












































































        ; lookup_method: more-detailed method lookup that returns a
        ; MethodMatch with mangled name, return type, and parameter
        ; types pre-computed. The `method` field of MethodMatch points
        ; at a PlannedFunction — we leave it null for now; wiring it up
        ; requires caching planned methods per struct, which we don't
        ; do yet.
        ; ================================================================
        ; Stage 7.2: compiler-generated region swizzle walkers.
        ;
        ; `rr.fix_graph(root)` — rr a pointer[ReceivedRegion], root a TYPED
        ; pointer into the received region — resolves to the generated
        ; symbol `_scaly_walk_<T>`, monomorphized per root type like the
        ; equals desugar. The planner validates the message type's
        ; transitive closure here (delegates and OS-resource types cannot
        ; cross the wire — hard diagnostics) and force-instantiates every
        ; reachable generic so the Emitter finds each layout; the Emitter
        ; generates the walker bodies on first call. A program that never
        ; calls fix_graph emits byte-identical IR.
        ; ================================================================

        ; Pointees the walker fixes but never descends into: page headers
        ; are swizzled by Region.deserialize itself; raw byte/void buffers
        ; carry no pointers (a function pointer smuggled through
        ; pointer[void] dies loudly in fix_value at runtime).
        function is_walker_header_name(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked
















        ; check_message_pointee: what a fixed pointer slot's TARGET needs.
        ; Scalars/void/const_char buffers and page headers are fix-only;
        ; aggregates recurse.
        procedure check_message_pointee(mutable this: Planner, mutable inner: PlannedType, loc: Span, mutable seen: ref[Array[String]]) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool io linked












        ; check_message_type: is `t` sound to swizzle with a generated
        ; walker? Appends a hard diagnostic and returns false when not.
        ; Also the instantiation-forcing pass: every reachable generic is
        ; instantiated so plan.structures/unions carry its layout for the
        ; Emitter's generator.
        procedure check_message_type(mutable this: Planner, mutable t: PlannedType, loc: Span, mutable seen: ref[Array[String]]) @page true @direct true @ret_nonlocal true returns bool io linked










































































































































































        ; resolve_fix_graph: synthesize the MethodMatch for the generated
        ; walker. Once the call SHAPE is right this always returns a match
        ; — closure violations are hard diagnostics that fail the compile
        ; at the end, so planning continues without a cascading
        ; "function not found".
        procedure resolve_fix_graph(
            mutable this: Planner,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[MethodMatch]? io linked













































































        procedure lookup_method(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable struct_type: PlannedType,
            mutable method_name: String,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[MethodMatch]? io linked









































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































        ; find_member_operator: resolve a member operator named `op_name`
        ; (`[]` for subscripts, `==`/`<>`/… for a concept's binary operators)
        ; to the receiver concept's declaration. Mirrors stage-0
        ; findSubscriptOperator (Planner.cpp:2896): bind the concept's generic
        ; params to the receiver's generic args (s167 rebuild so receiver
        ; bindings win under first-wins lookup), plan the operator under that
        ; substitution, and record it as a dispatchable PlannedFunction on
        ; first use so the Emitter defines the symbol (plan_structure's
        ; PlannedOperators are stored but never emitted). Returns null when
        ; the receiver's concept declares no matching operator — the caller
        ; then leaves the operand sequence untouched.
        procedure find_member_operator(
            mutable this: Planner,
            container_type: PlannedType,
            op_name: String,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand],
            loc: Span
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[MethodMatch]? io linked















































































































































































































































































































































































        ; generate_page_get_this: synthesize a `Page.get(this)` call,
        ; returned as a PlannedOperand whose result_type is
        ; `pointer[Page]`. Used when lowering a `^this` lifetime
        ; annotation — the emitter needs the page that currently owns
        ; `this` to allocate a sibling value.
        ;
        ; `this` must be a local in the current scope; the unreachable
        ; fallback returns a void placeholder so the type checker still
        ; threads a value through.
        procedure generate_page_get_this(mutable this: Planner, mutable loc: Span) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedOperand io linked


























































        ; s184: a construction result can carry a trailing member chain
        ; (`Point(3, 4).x`). The construction gate attaches the chain raw
        ; and keeps result_type = the struct type, so plan_tuple's
        ; single-component lift typed the whole group as the struct and
        ; emit_tuple rebuilt the struct from the extracted field. Resolve
        ; the hops against the operand's result type and rewrite chain +
        ; result_type when every hop resolves as a property — the same
        ; rule as the s175 call-branch fix. Returns the operand
        ; unchanged when the chain is absent or not all-property.
        procedure rebind_trailing_chain(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable operand: PlannedOperand
        ) @page true @direct true returns PlannedOperand io linked























































































        ; ================================================================
        ; resolve_member_access: walk a chain of member names against
        ; `base_type`, producing a PlannedMemberAccess for each hop.
        ;
        ; At each step:
        ;   - if the name is a property, record the planned PropertyType
        ;     and continue the chain from that type;
        ;   - else if the name is a method, record the planned return
        ;     type and continue from that type;
        ;   - else record a placeholder with void result (caller will
        ;     surface this as an error when emission is attempted).
        ;
        ; The produced access chain feeds `PlannedOperand.member_access`.
        ; ================================================================
        procedure resolve_member_access(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable base_type: PlannedType,
            mutable members: Vector[String]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns Vector[PlannedMemberAccess] io linked











































































































































































































































































































































































































































































        ; fresh_type_var: allocate a new type variable as a PlannedType
        ; wrapper with `variable` populated. Equivalent to Planner.cpp's
        ; freshTypeVar. `debug_name` is stored on the TypeVariable for
        ; error messages ("expected T, got int").
        procedure fresh_type_var(mutable this: Planner, debug_name: String) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedType linked

















        ; ================================================================
        ; Constraint management.
        ; ================================================================

        procedure add_equality_constraint(
            mutable this: Planner,
            left: PlannedType,
            right: PlannedType,
            loc: Span
        ) @persists true @ret_nonlocal true linked






        ; solve_and_apply: drain the constraint queue, unify everything,
        ; stash the result in `solution`. Returns true on success, false
        ; if any constraint failed to unify. Constraints are cleared on
        ; success so subsequent add_equality_constraint / solve cycles
        ; start fresh.
        procedure solve_and_apply(mutable this: Planner) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked





























        ; apply_current_substitution: rewrite `t` using the current
        ; `solution`. Convenience wrapper around apply_substitution so
        ; callers don't have to marshal the Array-as-Vector themselves.
        function apply_current_substitution(this: Planner, t: PlannedType) @page true @direct true returns PlannedType linked








        ; ================================================================
        ; plan_type: Model.Type → PlannedType using the active generic
        ; substitutions. Pulls together resolve_type_base (Model → Plan
        ; skeleton), apply_name_substitution (T → int, etc.), and the
        ; concept registry to fill in mangled_name for registered types.
        ; ================================================================
        ; is_concept_parameter_name: is `name` declared as a type PARAMETER by
        ; any registered concept? The tree's own generics all use a single
        ; uppercase letter, which is_generic_type_param_name# recognises
        ; directly and which type_mentions_generic_param# already depends on —
        ; but nothing in the language requires that, and a user writing
        ; `define Box[Elem]` must not have `Elem` reported as an unknown type
        ; while the TEMPLATE body is planned (there the parameter is unbound by
        ; definition). Asking every concept rather than the enclosing one is
        ; deliberately lax in the safe direction: it can only ever SUPPRESS a
        ; report, and the set it suppresses is the set of parameter names, which
        ; in this tree is T/K/V.
        ;
        ; Only reached from the unknown-name exit of plan_type#, so the scan is
        ; not on any hot path.
        function is_concept_parameter_name(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked























        ; unknown_type_name: `t` names nothing that could be a type — return that
        ; name, otherwise "". The type-annotation counterpart of
        ; unknown_identifier_name#, and the last of the silent classes: an
        ; annotation that resolves to no type was DROPPED with rc 0 and not a
        ; word, in every position that takes one —
        ;
        ;   let r: nosuchtype 5          function f(a: nosuchtype)
        ;   function f() returns nosuchtype   define R (a: nosuchtype)
        ;   mutable g: nosuchtype 3
        ;
        ; — and, worst of the set, `let r nosuchname + 1`, where the untyped
        ; binding PEELS its first operand as a type annotation, so a typo in an
        ; expression became a binding of an unknown type and the diagnostic the
        ; binary-operator check would have raised never ran.
        ;
        ; The exemptions, each for a shape that legitimately reaches the
        ; unknown-name exit of plan_type#:
        ;   * a DOTTED name — the namespace machinery owns those, and the
        ;     union-variant and namespace-function forms return before the exit;
        ;     the same carve-out unknown_identifier_name# makes.
        ;   * a primitive (a plain one returns early with a mangled name; this is
        ;     belt and braces for the array/generic rewrites).
        ;   * a generic type PARAMETER, by the single-letter convention and, for
        ;     user code that does not follow it, by is_concept_parameter_name#.
        ;   * any registered concept, asked WITHOUT use-visibility
        ;     (lookup_concept_any#): a type hidden by visibility is a different
        ;     diagnosis with its own hint, and reporting it here would make this
        ;     check a function of which `use` lines a file happens to carry.
        ; report_unknown_type: the ANNOTATION-position half of the check. Called
        ; only from the places a Model.Type arrives as a written annotation —
        ; plan_item# (binding, parameter, lambda parameter, init/operator
        ; parameter), plan_property#, plan_function#'s return and throws types,
        ; plan_global#, and plan_binding#'s peeled type.
        ;
        ; ★It is NOT called from plan_type#'s own unknown-name exit, and the
        ; measurement that ruled that out is worth keeping: `plan_type` is
        ; applied to any Type-SHAPED expression, not to annotations, so that exit
        ; reports 10535 times over this tree — 2068 of them the operator `+`,
        ; 1714 `*`, then `memcpy`, `exit`, `get_iterator`, `const_char`. That is
        ; the same reason unknown_identifier_name# carries its long exemption
        ; list. An annotation position needs no such list because a name there
        ; can only be a type.
        procedure report_unknown_type(mutable this: Planner, t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true linked

















        function unknown_type_name(this: Planner, t: PlannedType) @page true @direct true @ret_param_1 true returns String linked

















        ; report_unknown_page_name#: a `^name` page sigil must NAME something.
        ; `Foo^nosuchname(7)` used to compile in TOTAL SILENCE and take the
        ; whole statement with it: the Emitter's resolve_reference_page# found
        ; nothing, the construction emitted no value, and the enclosing
        ; statement was dropped -- a function whose body was one such binding
        ; came out as a bare `ret i64 0`, rc 0, not a word. Same class as the
        ; unknown-identifier and unknown-assignment-target drops.
        ; ★It cost a measuring round on 2026-08-26: `let pa RecA^page(7)`
        ; inside a function with no `page` in scope answered 0 for every field,
        ; which reads exactly like a broken union payload -- the probe it was
        ; written for was diagnosing something else entirely.
        ; Accepted without a scope entry:
        ;   this      the receiver's own page (the emitter wraps Page.get(this))
        ;   rp / _rp  the caller-page slot, peeled by the Modeler and
        ;             SYNTHESIZED by this planner (Lifetime.Reference("rp")),
        ;             so it legitimately has no binding of its own
        ; plus a PROPERTY of the receiver concept -- the `^field` form the
        ; emitter resolves through `this` (opensp's `^host` where host is a
        ; member). The receiver is read off `this`'s OWN binding, because the
        ; planner keeps no current-concept the way the emitter does.
        procedure report_unknown_page_name(mutable this: Planner, t: Type) linked

















































































        ; ================================================================
        ; TYPE ALIAS resolution at the MODEL level
        ; ================================================================
        ; Rewrite a Model Type tree, replacing every alias name by its target,
        ; BEFORE resolve_type_base# runs. That placement is the whole point:
        ; resolve_type_base# joins the dotted name, recurses on its generic
        ; arguments THROUGH ITSELF and builds the Itanium mangling -- it is
        ; static, has no Planner state and so cannot look a concept up. An alias
        ; nested inside `Array[LLVMValueRef]` is therefore never handed to
        ; plan_type# at all and cannot be resolved there.
        ; ★MEASURED, not reasoned: an env-gated probe over a converted scalyc
        ; reported 28 427 `…Ref` types reaching plan_type#, EVERY one of them
        ; with the flag armed and no generics -- i.e. every type plan_type# sees
        ; is already resolved, and the survivors were the ones it never saw.
        ; The tell in the diagnostics is a field declared with the alias
        ; (`Option[ref[Array[LLVMValueRef]]]`) taking a value whose type has
        ; resolved (`Array[pointer[LLVMOpaqueValue]]`).
        function model_type_has_alias(this: Planner, t: Type, depth: int) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked































        ; The rewrite itself. Only ever called where model_type_has_alias# said
        ; yes, so the common path allocates nothing.
        function resolve_model_type_aliases(this: Planner, t: Type, depth: int) @page true @direct true returns Type linked































        procedure plan_type(mutable this: Planner, mutable t: Type) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedType io linked












































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































        ; ================================================================
        ; plan_item: Model.Item → PlannedItem.
        ;
        ; Handles the optional name and type annotation. Attributes are
        ; dropped for now — the PlannedItem carries `null` in that slot
        ; until attribute planning is ported.
        ;
        ; Returns-by-value copies the PlannedItem to the caller; the refs
        ; inside (name, item_type) point into single-element holder Vectors
        ; allocated on this function's rp, so they survive the caller's
        ; use of the returned PlannedItem.
        ; ================================================================
        procedure plan_item(mutable this: Planner, mutable i: Item) @page true @direct true returns PlannedItem io linked














































        procedure plan_item_attributes(mutable this: Planner, attributes: Vector[Attribute]) @ret_param_0 true returns ref[Vector[PlannedAttribute]] linked
















        ; plan_items: batch version of plan_item over a Vector[Item].
        ; Pre-allocates the result Vector at the correct size so we can
        ; use `put(i, ...)` instead of Array.add + copy.
        procedure plan_items(mutable this: Planner, mutable items: Vector[Item]) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns Vector[PlannedItem] io linked

















        ; plan_constant_operand: wrap a Constant literal as a PlannedOperand
        ; with its inferred type populated. The result_type is whatever
        ; infer_constant_type says at pre-solver time (e.g. IntegerConstant
        ; → "int", StringConstant → String).
        function plan_constant_operand(
            this: Planner,
            c: Constant,
            loc: Span
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedOperand linked










        ; ================================================================
        ; Simple Expression planners.
        ; ================================================================

        ; plan_sizeof: SizeOf(type) → PlannedSizeOf with computed byte
        ; size. get_primitive_alignment doubles as size for primitives
        ; but returns the catch-all 8 for any non-primitive — wrong for
        ; struct types like Module (80 B). Look the Concept up in the
        ; registry (set up during plan_program's namespace walk, before
        ; generic instantiation runs) and compute the size from its
        ; Model.Property list. Each property's contribution is either a
        ; primitive byte size or a hardcoded stdlib-container shape:
        ; Vector = 16 (length + data), String/HashMap = 8 (single ptr).
        ; That gives Module = 1 (bool) + 7 (pad) + 2*8 + 3*16 + 8 = 80,
        ; matching the LLVM struct layout. Without this, Vector(rp,len)
        ; for Vector[Module] allocates len*8 instead of len*80, and
        ; put(i, m) overruns and SEGVs build_program.
        procedure plan_sizeof(mutable this: Planner, mutable s: SizeOf) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedSizeOf io linked

















        ; plan_is: Is(name) is a runtime variant-tag test. At this site
        ; we resolve the test type and (best-effort) the union_name; the
        ; `variant_tag` and `value` fields get stitched in at the
        ; operand-sequence collapse site, which has the value operand.
        procedure plan_is(mutable this: Planner, mutable i: Is) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedIs io linked








































        ; plan_as: `x as TargetType` — explicit cast. Resolves the
        ; target type; the value operand is stitched in at the use site
        ; (typically during collapse, like PlannedIs).
        procedure plan_as(mutable this: Planner, mutable a: scaly.compiler.Model.As) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedAs io linked





        ; plan_alignof: alignof(Type) → size_t. Mirrors plan_sizeof.
        ; s106: take the alignment from get_primitive_alignment instead
        ; of hardcoding 0. Without this, every `alignof T` lowers to a
        ; literal 0 — observed first as init_lifetime's
        ; `page.allocate(sizeof int, alignof int)` passing align=0.
        procedure plan_alignof(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable a: scaly.compiler.Model.AlignOf
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedAlignOf io linked






        ; plan_type_operand: a Model.Type used as an operand can mean
        ; qualified_ref_is_dangling: a VALUE-position qualified name `X.Y`
        ; (exactly two segments) whose prefix X is a known NAMESPACE but whose
        ; suffix Y is not any member of it (function/operator/nested concept),
        ; not a registered concept/type (X.Y or bare Y), not a union variant,
        ; and not a global constant. Such a reference is a dangling qualified
        ; access — a typo like `NS.bogus` — that currently plans leniently as a
        ; bare Type operand (the emitter then produces garbage: `return NS.bogus`
        ; compiles and returns 0). This lets plan_type_operand#'s value fallback
        ; hard-error instead. Deliberately CONSERVATIVE — it fires only for a
        ; two-segment name with a resolvable NAMESPACE prefix, so deep paths,
        ; package/module names, type variables, generic params, struct static
        ; calls, and every real member are left untouched (no false positive).
        ; A parenless reference to a REAL member (`NS.real`, function not called)
        ; is NOT flagged — that is a separate lenient case, out of scope here.
        procedure qualified_ref_is_dangling(mutable this: Planner, t: Type) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked

























































































        ; different things depending on whether the name resolves to:
        ;   - a local variable in scope → PlannedVariable with its type
        ;   - a top-level function name → keep as Type, result is a
        ;     function reference (return-type filled in later during
        ;     call resolution)
        ;   - an actual type name → PlannedExpression.Type with the
        ;     planned type
        procedure plan_type_operand(mutable this: Planner, mutable t: Type, loc: Span) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedOperand io linked


















































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































        ; ================================================================
        ; Control-flow planners.
        ; ================================================================

        ; statement_result_type: extract the PlannedType produced by a
        ; statement used as an expression. Covers the Action case (the
        ; common `if cond x else y` shape); non-value statements
        ; (Return/Throw/Break/Continue/Binding) return void.
        function statement_result_type(s: PlannedStatement) @page true @direct true returns PlannedType linked








        ; plan_if: plans the condition (a Vector[Operand]), the consequent
        ; statement, and optionally the alternative. Emits a bool
        ; constraint on the condition and (when an alternative exists)
        ; unifies the two branches' result types.
        procedure plan_if(mutable this: Planner, mutable f: If) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedIf io linked

































































































































        ; plan_lambda: anonymous function literal. Opens a fresh scope,
        ; seeds each parameter with a fresh type variable (lambdas omit
        ; parameter type annotations), plans the body Action, and
        ; synthesizes a unique mangled name. Captures are the set of
        ; variable names referenced in the body that aren't lambda
        ; parameters — a best-effort over-approximation (inner bindings
        ; may leak in) that the emitter can intersect with the real
        ; outer scope.
        ; collect_free_names: walk a PlannedOperand's expression tree
        ; and accumulate the names of every PlannedVariable reference
        ; encountered. Recurses into Call args, Tuple components, and
        ; If/Match/Block/Choose sub-statements. Used by plan_lambda's
        ; capture-discovery pass — captures are this set minus any
        ; lambda-local names (params + inner bindings).
        procedure collect_free_names(
            this: Planner,
            op: PlannedOperand,
            mutable acc: ref[Array[String]]
        ) @ret_param_2 true linked






















































        ; Recursive lambda-body name walkers used by monomorphize_lambda#'s
        ; capture discovery. Unlike collect_free_names (which only reaches
        ; Variable/Call/Tuple, so a block-bodied lambda's captures were missed
        ; entirely), these recurse through every compound expression and
        ; statement. They are FLOW-SENSITIVE within a linear statement
        ; sequence: `bound` is maintained in evaluation order, and a name a
        ; body PlannedBinding (or a for-loop / when / try identifier)
        ; introduces is added to `bound` ONLY AFTER its initializer is walked.
        ; A PlannedVariable USE is recorded into `uses` (a capture candidate)
        ; only when its name is not yet in `bound` at that point — so a use
        ; that lexically PRECEDES a body binding of the same name (the
        ; use-before-bind case `let n n + 1` over an enclosing `n`) captures
        ; the enclosing value, while a use AFTER the binding is the local.
        ; Ordinary shadowing (`let n 3; x + n`, no preceding use) stays local.
        ; The walk is also SCOPE-ACCURATE, not just linear: every construct
        ; that pushes a planner scope (block, for/while/repeat body, choose /
        ; match / try when-arm, try-else) records the bound-set length on entry
        ; and TRUNCATES back on exit (`set bound.length:` — `bound` is passed by
        ; reference), so a name bound only inside a nested block or sibling
        ; branch does NOT suppress a capture of an enclosing same-named variable
        ; used after it. `if` branches, choose/match `else`, and the try-bound
        ; name are NOT scoped/truncated here because plan_if/plan_choose/
        ; plan_match don't push a scope for them and the try binding leaks into
        ; the enclosing scope — mirroring the planner exactly keeps "bound at
        ; this point" equal to "pass-1 resolved this use to a body-local".
        function name_in_string_array(this: Planner, arr: Array[String], name: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked













        procedure collect_lambda_names_ops(this: Planner, ops_ref: ref[Vector[PlannedOperand]]?, mutable uses: ref[Array[String]], mutable bound: ref[Array[String]]) linked














        procedure collect_lambda_names_action(this: Planner, act: PlannedAction, mutable uses: ref[Array[String]], mutable bound: ref[Array[String]]) @ret_nonlocal true linked





        procedure collect_lambda_names_stmt(this: Planner, stmt: PlannedStatement, mutable uses: ref[Array[String]], mutable bound: ref[Array[String]]) @ret_nonlocal true linked
























        procedure collect_lambda_names_stmts(this: Planner, stmts_ref: ref[Vector[PlannedStatement]]?, mutable uses: ref[Array[String]], mutable bound: ref[Array[String]]) linked














        procedure collect_lambda_names_stmt_opt(this: Planner, stmt_ref: ref[PlannedStatement]?, mutable uses: ref[Array[String]], mutable bound: ref[Array[String]]) linked






        procedure collect_lambda_names(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand, mutable uses: ref[Array[String]], mutable bound: ref[Array[String]]) @ret_nonlocal true linked






















































































































































































































        procedure plan_lambda(mutable this: Planner, mutable lam: Lambda) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedLambda io linked































































































































        ; Phase-1 lambda monomorphization: given a lambda model and the
        ; call-site argument types, synthesize (and record, once) a concrete
        ; top-level function whose parameters take those arg types, plan its
        ; body, and return the function's mangled name. The call site then
        ; resolves to a direct call to this function (see the lambda-call
        ; intercept in collapse_operand_sequence#). Cached per mangled name
        ; via has_recorded_function so repeated calls with identical arg
        ; types reuse the same function.
        procedure monomorphize_lambda(mutable this: Planner, base_name: String, mutable lam: Lambda, arg_types: Vector[PlannedType]) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns LambdaMonoResult io linked








































































































































































































































        ; GAP B substep 2: build a closure VALUE for a lambda assigned to a
        ; delegate type. Monomorphizes the real lambda against the delegate's
        ; parameter types (captures become leading params, reused from
        ; monomorphize_lambda#), then packages a PlannedClosure carrying the
        ; real symbol, a generated thunk symbol, the closure record type name,
        ; and the captured VALUES (looked up here, where they are in scope) +
        ; their types. The emitter lowers it to a heap `{ptr thunk, caps...}`
        ; record; the thunk bridges the uniform `R(ptr self, params)` call
        ; shape to `real(caps..., params)`. `life` is the record's allocation
        ; lifetime (local region, or `#` caller page when the closure escapes).
        procedure make_closure(mutable this: Planner, base_name: String, mutable lam: Lambda, dparams: Vector[PlannedType], dret: PlannedType, del_pt: PlannedType, life: Lifetime) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedClosure io linked





















































        ; plan_repeat: do-while body runs at least once; the terminating
        ; condition lives inside the body action via `break` or an
        ; embedded if. Nothing else to constrain at this level.
        procedure plan_repeat(mutable this: Planner, mutable r: Repeat) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedRepeat io linked











        ; Recursive break-value-type finder. Descends through if/choose/match
        ; consequents and nested blocks, but NOT into nested while/for/repeat
        ; bodies (an unlabeled break there targets the inner loop, not this
        ; repeat). Returns void when no break-with-value is found.
        function find_break_type_in_operands(this: Planner, ops: ref[Vector[PlannedOperand]]?) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedType linked



















        function find_break_type_in_statement(this: Planner, s: PlannedStatement) @page true @direct true returns PlannedType linked




















        function find_break_type_in_stmt_ref(this: Planner, s: ref[PlannedStatement]?) @page true @direct true returns PlannedType linked






        function find_break_type_in_expr(this: Planner, e: PlannedExpression) @page true @direct true returns PlannedType linked
































































































        ; plan_while: Binding condition + Action body. The binding opens
        ; a short-lived scope for the loop variable. Emits a constraint
        ; that the planned binding's type is bool (the solver promotes
        ; `while let maybe_x = ...` through Option's None sentinel, so
        ; this is a reasonable approximation today).
        procedure plan_while(mutable this: Planner, mutable w: While) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedWhile io linked














































































































        ; plan_for: loop variable bound to each element of `expression`.
        ; Two shapes are supported, matching the C++ Planner:
        ;   1. Integer range: `for i in N` — element type == N's type.
        ;   2. Iterator protocol: `for x in collection` — resolve
        ;      `get_iterator` on the collection type, then `next` on the
        ;      iterator type; the element type is what `next` returns.
        ; Option[T] and ref/pointer wrappers on the collection type are
        ; peeled before the dispatch.
        procedure plan_for(mutable this: Planner, mutable f: For) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedFor io linked























































































































































































































        ; try_condition_can_throw#: does ANY operand of a try's condition throw?
        ;
        ; plan_try below reads only the LAST operand, because that is where the
        ; Result wrapper it has to unpack sits. For the DIAGNOSIS the question
        ; is the other one -- is there anything in this condition that could
        ; throw at all -- and it must be asked over every operand, or a
        ; condition like `f() + 1` with a throwing `f` would be reported as a
        ; lie when it is not one.
        function try_condition_can_throw(bind: PlannedBinding) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked


        ; try_fault_arm_count#: how many arms name a RuntimeFault variant?
        ; The COUNT and not a yes/no, so the diagnosis can tell a try that
        ; catches NOTHING from one that MIXES a fault arm with a foreign one --
        ; two different mistakes, and the second one's message was misleading
        ; while both said "the when arms are unreachable".
        procedure try_fault_arm_count(mutable this: Planner, t: Try) @ret_nonlocal true returns size_t linked






















        ; try_arms_are_faults#: does EVERY arm of this try name a variant of
        ; the prelude's RuntimeFault?
        ;
        ; ★Every arm, not any: a try that mixes a fault arm with something else
        ; is a confusion, and answering "yes" would send the other arm into the
        ; panic path where its tag matches nothing. A mixed try keeps the hard
        ; diagnosis below instead.
        procedure try_arms_are_faults(mutable this: Planner, t: Try) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked
































        ; report_try_without_throw#: a `try` whose condition cannot throw.
        ;
        ; ★★★It used to compile in TOTAL SILENCE and do nothing: the `when`
        ; arms were not emitted at all -- `try let x s[i] when e: Oob return 55`
        ; ended the process with rc 15 and the 55 never appeared in the IR, and
        ; `try let v plain(argc) when e: X` simply ran the call and dropped the
        ; arm (both measured 2026-09-07 on the production binary). So an author
        ; could write a handled case that is none, and nothing anywhere said a
        ; word.
        ;
        ; ★It is the CONDITION that is judged, never the arms: a `try` with no
        ; arms at all is left alone, and so is one whose condition throws
        ; somewhere other than in its last operand.
        procedure report_try_without_throw(mutable this: Planner, t: Try, bind: PlannedBinding) linked





















        ; report_unhandled_throw#: a binding whose operation calls a `throws`
        ; function, outside any try.
        ;
        ; ★★★It used to be SILENT and wrong, which is worse than the try class
        ; closed the same day: `let x step(b)` where step throws answered
        ; 6164131296 -- the Result wrapper read as the success value -- with no
        ; diagnostic and no trap. And it did not propagate either: the same
        ; line inside a function that itself declares `throws` took the success
        ; arm, so the error vanished between two functions that both say
        ; `throws` and the caller's honest `choose ... when e: Error` saw a
        ; success with a garbage value (measured 2026-09-07, TRAPS.md 3.9).
        ;
        ; The tree's convention is EXPLICIT propagation -- CLAUDE.md's "no
        ; exceptions, explicit error propagation", and
        ; tests/regress/throws_propagate.scaly writes `choose` plus `throw e`.
        ; What was missing is that leaving it out said nothing.
        ;
        ; ★Judged at the BINDING, because that is where the wrapper is read as
        ; a value. A `choose` over the call handles it (its subject is an
        ; operation, not a binding), and a `try` plans its binding through
        ; plan_try rather than through plan_statement, so neither reaches here.
        ; ops_contain_throwing_call#: the same question as
        ; try_condition_can_throw#, but over a bare operand sequence -- a
        ; `return` and a `set` carry no PlannedBinding.
        function ops_contain_throwing_call(ops: ref[Vector[PlannedOperand]]?) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked




































        procedure report_unhandled_throw_ops(mutable this: Planner, ops: ref[Vector[PlannedOperand]]?, loc: Span) linked










        procedure report_unhandled_throw(mutable this: Planner, bind: PlannedBinding) linked










        ; plan_try: try { binding } catch WhenPatterns else alt.
        procedure plan_try(mutable this: Planner, mutable t: Try) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedTry io linked






















































































        ; plan_when: one pattern arm of choose/try. When `union_type` is
        ; non-null and names a registered union concept, the variant's
        ; payload type and tag index are resolved so the Emitter can
        ; extract the payload and compare tags correctly. When union_type
        ; is void (the fallback from plan_try with no exception type),
        ; the arm still plans but variant_type stays void and tag stays 0.
        procedure plan_when(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable w: When,
            mutable union_type: PlannedType,
            is_throws_call: bool,
            throws_ty: ref[PlannedType]?
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedWhen io linked

































































































































































































        ; plan_choose: Scaly's choose/when pattern match.
        ; Phase-5 class 2 helpers (choose-payload back-prop). True when ops
        ; is exactly one bare single-segment name equal to `name`.
        function ops_are_bare_name(ops: Vector[Operand], name: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked
























        function binding_flows_payload_to_escaping(this: Planner, b: scalyc.compiler.Model.Binding, payload: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked













        function action_flows_payload_to_escaping(this: Planner, a: Action, payload: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked

























































        function stmt_flows_payload_to_escaping(this: Planner, s: Statement, payload: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked











        ; Phase-5 class 2: does this when-arm's consequent store the arm
        ; payload BARE into a local that the completed escape sets mark
        ; escaping (`when prog: Success set syntax: prog`, where `syntax`
        ; later feeds the returned Module)? Field-read sources (`ok.text`)
        ; are not covered in this increment — the compiler's shapes store
        ; the payload bare.
        function when_payload_flows_to_escaping(this: Planner, w: When) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked




        procedure plan_choose(mutable this: Planner, mutable c: Choose) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedChoose io linked























































































































































































        ; plan_case / plan_branch / plan_match: the full match-expression form.
        procedure plan_case(mutable this: Planner, mutable cs: Case) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedCase io linked






        procedure plan_branch(mutable this: Planner, mutable b: Branch) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedBranch io linked




















        procedure plan_match(mutable this: Planner, mutable m: Match) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedMatch io linked













































































































        ; ================================================================
        ; Tuples and matrices.
        ; ================================================================

        ; plan_component: Model.Component → PlannedComponent.
        ; Mirrors Planner.cpp:10499-10512 — each component's operand
        ; sequence is collapsed so prefix ops like `*p` and method-call
        ; arguments resolve against the collapsed result rather than the
        ; raw operand sequence. emit_tuple's grouping shortcut emits
        ; comp.value[0], so without this collapse `(*p).field` would
        ; emit only the leading `Type("*")` operand and produce null.
        procedure plan_component(mutable this: Planner, mutable c: Component) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedComponent io linked



































































































































































































        procedure plan_tuple(mutable this: Planner, mutable t: Tuple) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedTuple io linked























































        procedure plan_matrix(mutable this: Planner, mutable m: Matrix) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns scalyc.compiler.Plan.PlannedMatrix io linked
























        ; plan_new: `new Foo(args)` — object construction. The mangled
        ; initializer and resolved struct type depend on the concept
        ; table, so we plan the type skeleton and arguments and leave
        ; the call layout for later resolution. For now we model it as
        ; a PlannedTuple carrying the argument list and the planned type.
        procedure plan_new(mutable this: Planner, mutable n: New) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedTuple io linked





















































        ; ================================================================
        ; plan_operand: dispatch by Expression variant, then wrap with
        ; any member-access chain declared on the operand.
        ; ================================================================
        procedure plan_operand(mutable this: Planner, mutable op: scalyc.compiler.Model.Operand) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedOperand io linked













































































































































































































        ; plan_operands: batch plan_operand over a Vector.
        ; s184: unit variant referenced without an argument tuple —
        ; `Option.None` as a value. Mirrors stage-0's planOperands
        ; Case 3 (Planner.cpp:8376-8419): a two-segment name whose
        ; first segment is a registered union and whose second a
        ; payload-less variant plans as a PlannedVariantConstruction
        ; with no value. Returns null when the shape doesn't match so
        ; the caller falls back to plan_operand#. Locals win — a
        ; shadowing local binding disqualifies the union lookup.
        procedure try_plan_unit_variant(
            mutable this: Planner,
            t: Type,
            loc: Span
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns ref[PlannedOperand]? linked





































































        procedure plan_operands(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable ops: Vector[scalyc.compiler.Model.Operand]
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns Vector[PlannedOperand] io linked






















































        ; ================================================================
        ; plan_binding: Model.Binding → Plan.PlannedBinding.
        ;
        ; Responsibilities:
        ;   1. Plan the initializer operands (by calling plan_operands).
        ;   2. Plan the binding's annotated item, which yields the
        ;      PlannedItem with an optional PlannedType.
        ;   3. Register the binding in the current scope so later uses
        ;      resolve by name.
        ;
        ; plan_binding now collapses the operation sequence. Stack-array
        ; type detection (`[T, N]` at binding site) is deferred.
        ; ================================================================

        ; Drop a LEADING `=` from a binding initializer (the assignment
        ; form `let x = value` → operands `[=, value]`). `=` is also the
        ; comparison operator, so a NON-leading `=` (`let r a = b`) must be
        ; kept — it collapses to the comparison `r = (a == b)`. Mirrors the
        ; stage-0 index-0 if in Planner.cpp::planBinding.
        function strip_leading_eq(ops: Vector[PlannedOperand]) @page true @direct true returns Vector[PlannedOperand] linked































        procedure plan_binding(mutable this: Planner, mutable b: Binding) @page true @direct true returns PlannedBinding io linked



















































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































        ; ================================================================
        ; plan_action / plan_return / plan_break / plan_throw.
        ;
        ; Each wraps a Vector[Operand] into the appropriate Planned*
        ; variant. plan_action collapses its source + target operand
        ; sequences via collapse_operand_sequence (which folds binary
        ; ops, method chains, is/as, and prefix ops); result_type takes
        ; the collapsed source's type.
        ; ================================================================

        ; unknown_identifier_name: when a collapsed operand is a bare,
        ; chain-free, SINGLE-segment Type expression that names nothing
        ; reachable — not a local (that would have planned as Variable),
        ; module global, function (a bare top-level function name is a
        ; legal function-pointer value), concept, delegate, operator,
        ; primitive, or generic type parameter — return that name;
        ; otherwise "". Such an operand emits as null downstream and used
        ; to drop the enclosing statement SILENTLY. Dotted names keep the
        ; lenient path (namespace machinery owns them).
        procedure unknown_identifier_name(mutable this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @page true @direct true returns String linked

















































































        ; The exemption battery of unknown_identifier_name#, as its own
        ; question so the bare name and the head of a dotted one are judged by
        ; ONE rule. (A name declared two ways is exempt either way: this
        ; answers "something in scope carries this name", not "which".)
        ; (a procedure, not a function: the lookups memoize through `this`)
        procedure name_resolves_to_something(mutable this: Planner, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked


























        ; ====================================================================
        ; ★★★`&fn` ON A FUNCTION THAT TAKES AN IMPLICIT CALLER PAGE (2026-09-20).
        ; The address-of path mangles with the page the source DECLARES; the
        ; definition's name comes from function_needs_caller_page#, which also
        ; asks the BODY. Widen one Option deep in the call graph and the
        ; definition silently becomes `_Z9jit_applyPN4scaly6memory4PageEPvPv`
        ; while `&jit_apply` still emits `_Z9jit_applyPvPv` -- and the ONLY
        ; evidence is `ld: symbol(s) not found`, with no line and no relation to
        ; the change that caused it.
        ;
        ; ★★★Why a DIAGNOSTIC and not a corrected mangling, which is the obvious
        ; fix and is WORSE: the helpers this bites are pinned to a `(void*,
        ; void*)` C ABI by the code that calls them through the pointer, so a
        ; page-taking definition is an ABI break whatever it is called. The
        ; undefined symbol is the guard; this turns it into a sentence.
        ;
        ; ★The exit for a real case is to keep the body ALLOCATION-FREE: an NPO
        ; return falls through to body_may_allocate#, so splitting a cold
        ; reporting half into its own function is usually all it takes.
        ; ====================================================================
        procedure report_address_of_page_function(mutable this: Planner, mutable f: Function, loc: Span) io linked













        ; report_unknown_identifier: the same message the four inline
        ; unknown-name checks build (binary operand, `set` target, `set` source,
        ; binding initializer) — name, use-visibility hint, report. A helper
        ; rather than a fifth copy because the CALL ARGUMENT site sits inside
        ; collapse_operand_sequence# and must stay a SINGLE statement: an inline
        ; branch there perturbs the s178 alloca pool (see
        ; resolve_static_method_on# for the same reasoning). The four inline
        ; copies are deliberately left alone — rewriting them buys nothing and
        ; each one would have to be re-proved emission-neutral.
        ;
        ; The argument position was the one place a name that resolves to
        ; NOTHING travelled without a word. `Box.twice(nosuchname, 3)` and
        ; `x.twice(nosuchname)` both planned with rc 0 and passed whatever the
        ; slot held; on a free function the same typo was reported only
        ; INDIRECTLY, as "function not found: twice" — the unknown argument
        ; gets a fresh type variable, no overload matches, and the message
        ; names the callee instead of the typo. Reporting at the argument
        ; makes all three shapes say the same true thing.
        ; ── Arity of a call that PREPENDS ITS RECEIVER (2026-08-15).
        ;
        ; A dotted call on a value receiver passes the receiver as argument 0, so
        ; the receiver always occupies ONE parameter — but WHICH one depends on
        ; the member:
        ;
        ;   `s.length()`        declares `this`  -> receiver binds `this`
        ;   `old.is_equal(gid)` this-LESS        -> receiver fills its FIRST
        ;                                           declared parameter, and gid
        ;                                           fills the second
        ;                                           (dazzle/ELObj.scaly:970)
        ;
        ; ★★★ WHY THIS CANNOT LIVE IN lookup_method#, where the other arity gate
        ; is: that function serves a THIRD shape it cannot tell from the second —
        ; a this-less member called UNQUALIFIED from a sibling
        ; (`allocate_page()` inside Page.allocate_exclusive_page), where nothing
        ; is prepended and args = params exactly. Its gate therefore accepts both
        ; readings, and that disjunction is what let the defect through.
        ; Tightening it there was tried and REFUTED by the build: the stdlib's own
        ; Page.allocate_page / Page.release_page sibling calls fail it. The
        ; distinguishing fact — is there a receiver at all — exists only at the
        ; call site, which is here.
        ;
        ; What was silent before: `this.one(21)` against a this-less
        ; `one(c: int)` planned with rc 0 and emitted
        ; `call @one(ptr this, i64 21)` into a ONE-parameter function. The
        ; receiver landed in `c`, the real argument was dropped, and emit_call
        ; builds the callee type from the ARGUMENTS so llc saw nothing wrong
        ; either. Found porting the TypeScript scanner: a this-less predicate
        ; answered its own receiver's address, so an identifier scan never
        ; advanced and the loop spun until the process was killed.
        ;
        ; A SINGLE statement at the call site, and the work in here, because an
        ; inline branch inside collapse_operand_sequence# perturbs its s178
        ; alloca pool — the same discipline report_unknown_identifier# follows.
        ; Conservative on anything it cannot judge (no match, no recorded
        ; parameter types): silence beats a false diagnostic in a gate this
        ; broad.
        ; report_null_into_ref#: `ref[T]` is a BORROWED REFERENCE and the docs
        ; have always said so -- `docs/scaly/chapters/types.xml` reserves the
        ; nullable spelling `ref[T]?` for a slot that may hold nothing. Nothing
        ; enforced it, because `ref` and `pointer` are one representation and
        ; null flows into either; so the 2026-08-23 port conversion carried the
        ; ports' nullability across unchanged and 630-odd declarations began
        ; claiming a reference exists where the code stores null. This makes the
        ; claim checkable at the four positions where a TARGET TYPE is in hand.
        ;
        ; Deliberately narrow: it judges the bare `null` LITERAL against a
        ; declared `ref[T]`, not the general flow of an Option into a
        ; non-optional. The wider question -- may a `ref[T]?` be passed where a
        ; `ref[T]` is declared -- is a separate one and is still allowed, so
        ; this is a diagnostic about DECLARATIONS, not yet a non-null proof.
        ; An `Option[ref[T]]` target (the `?` spelling, Modeler s113) is exactly
        ; what the fix is, so it must never fire on one -- the name test does
        ; that, since the wrapper arrives named "Option".
        ;
        ; ONE statement at each call site: an inline branch in the hot planners
        ; perturbs the s178 alloca pool (six -g checks, measured).
        procedure report_null_into_ref(
            mutable this: Planner,
            target_type: PlannedType,
            source: PlannedOperand,
            loc: Span
        ) linked


















































        ; operand_is_null_through_casts#: a bare `null` LITERAL, seen through
        ; any number of `as` wrappers. `null as pointer[X]` is the same value
        ; and the same claim as `null` -- the cast relocates nothing and proves
        ; nothing -- so a gate that asks operand_is_null_constant# alone can be
        ; stepped around by spelling the cast, and the corpus PREFERRED that
        ; spelling: of the 85 argument-position findings swept on 2026-08-27,
        ; 21 were written `null as pointer[X]`. Same lesson as the trailing-`as`
        ; class in the page walkers: no question ABOUT a value may be answered
        ; by the cast sitting on top of it.
        function operand_is_null_through_casts(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked


















        ; report_null_into_ref_arg#: the ARGUMENT position -- the ONE place
        ; null still reaches a non-optional `ref[T]`, and the reason it does is
        ; deliberate everywhere else. `report_null_into_ref#` judges the four
        ; positions that carry a DECLARED type, and `type_conformance_accepted#`
        ; class 2 refuses an Option at those same four; an argument is judged by
        ; neither, because `null` plans as `pointer[void]` and types_compatible#
        ; accepts THAT for every pointer and every ref parameter BY INTENT --
        ; overload resolution rests on it. So the call resolves, plans, mangles
        ; and emits in silence, and the callee holds a reference its own
        ; declaration says cannot be null.
        ;
        ; Swept before the gate existed (tools/nullref/nullarg.py): 85 sites
        ; over 40 declarations, and 37 of the 40 NEVER TEST the parameter --
        ; they forward it -- which is why every body-reading sweep of the
        ; nullref campaign had missed the whole class. A forwarder tests
        ; nothing; only its call sites know.
        ;
        ; ★Alignment is copied from coerce_string_args_to_cstr# and must stay
        ; copied: param_types EXCLUDES `this` in every resolution path, so the
        ; user arguments are TAIL-aligned and a prepended receiver or implicit
        ; page argument is absorbed by the offset. The two guards are that
        ; discipline: nothing to say when there are no parameter types, and a
        ; shorter argument list than parameter list is an arity question the
        ; arity gates own.
        ;
        ; ★Narrow on purpose, in the same two ways report_null_into_ref# is: it
        ; judges a null LITERAL and not the general flow of an Option (which
        ; types_compatible# already refuses at a parameter, so every call site
        ; spells the `x as ref[X]` unwrap), and it never fires on the `?`
        ; spelling, since the s113 wrapper arrives named "Option".
        procedure report_null_into_ref_arg(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable args: Vector[PlannedOperand],
            param_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            callee: String
        ) linked


























































        ; report_null_into_ref_field#: the CONSTRUCTION arm of the argument
        ; gate, and it needs its own because a construction handed a bare
        ; `null` never reaches the call funnels at all. The init match is what
        ; diverts it: `null` plans as `pointer[void]` and types_compatible#
        ; accepts that for NO ref parameter, so `check_initializer_match`
        ; rejects the init and the construction falls through to the
        ; POSITIONAL TUPLE path -- where the argument lands in the field
        ; directly and no declared parameter is ever consulted. That is why
        ; `Rec(null)` was silent while `obj.m(null)` was not.
        ;
        ; ★It asks the ARGUMENT TYPE, not an operand, because this path has no
        ; operands -- and it can: the bare null literal IS the type
        ; `pointer[void]` mangled "Pv" (make_null_type), which is the same test
        ; the "no matching initializer" exemption thirty lines up already makes
        ; for the same reason. ★A `null` into an OPTIONAL field stays correct
        ; and stays silent: the s113 wrapper arrives named "Option", so the
        ; `ref` name test excludes it, exactly as in the other two gates.
        procedure report_null_into_ref_field(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable s: Structure,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            loc: Span
        ) io linked


























        ; The message half, split out for the reason report_null_into_ref#
        ; splits its own: the walk above stays one statement per branch.
        procedure report_null_arg(mutable this: Planner, param_type: PlannedType, loc: Span) @ret_nonlocal true linked
















        ; option_arg_into_plain_ref#: the OTHER half of the argument gate, and
        ; the half its own comment assumed away. report_null_into_ref_arg#
        ; judges a null LITERAL because "the general flow of an Option is
        ; refused by types_compatible# at a parameter" -- true on the FREE
        ; function path, where check_function_match# compares every argument,
        ; and FALSE on the method path: lookup_method# takes a single by-name
        ; candidate unexamined (its selection loop is gated on candidates > 1),
        ; and the gate that exists for exactly that reason,
        ; report_method_arg_types#, compares the pair only AFTER
        ; gate_strip_indirection# has taken the Option off one side and the ref
        ; off the other -- its own comment says the stripped pair "only ever
        ; ADDS acceptance", and an accepted null is what it adds.
        ;
        ; Measured 2026-08-28 with three probes one line apart: a literal
        ; `null` through a method is rc 4, an Option through a FREE function is
        ; rc 4, and an Option through a METHOD planned at rc 0 while the binary
        ; exited 139 -- a SIGSEGV at the first member hop, precisely the
        ; failure exit 21 was written to replace. The trap cannot fire there,
        ; because no unwrap is emitted where no conformance was ever demanded.
        ;
        ; The question is NULLABILITY ALONE, so the pointees must still agree:
        ; a genuine type mismatch is report_method_arg_types#'s to report, and
        ; telling its author to write an unwrap would be wrong advice.
        ;
        ; Ask the SHAPE, not the name -- both `ref[T]?` and `pointer[T]?`
        ; arrive as an Option around something pointer-shaped, and a name test
        ; that knows only "ref" is the standing bug of this seam.
        procedure option_arg_into_plain_ref(param_type: PlannedType, mutable arg_type: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked


















        ; The operand-level question: the type rule above, minus every name a
        ; null test has already PROVEN in this branch. Narrowing is a fact
        ; about the NAME (define_narrowed#), never a type change, so it can
        ; only be asked here -- and it is what separates a guarded forward
        ; from a lie. Measured on the untouched tree: 2459 sites report
        ; without it, 1222 of them behind a guard the author already wrote.
        procedure option_arg_reaches_plain_ref(mutable this: Planner, param_type: PlannedType, mutable arg: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked



























        ; The message names the unwrap, because there IS no flow narrowing of
        ; the TYPE: a caller who has just tested the value still owes the
        ; cast, and that cast is where the exit-21 trap is emitted.
        procedure report_option_arg(mutable this: Planner, param_type: PlannedType, arg_type: PlannedType, loc: Span, callee: String, index: size_t) @ret_nonlocal true linked























        ; report_type_mismatch#: does the value actually CONFORM to the type
        ; the declaration claims? Nothing asked. `current_return_type` and the
        ; binding/`set` target types were only ever READ -- for the literal
        ; retype, the char/float fold, the cstr wrap, the tail promotion and
        ; the three `ref` gates -- and never used as an ACCEPTOR, while
        ; types_compatible# ran at ten sites, every one of them a PARAMETER.
        ; So a `return` was unchecked in both directions. Measured on the
        ; untouched tree, all rc 0 and silent:
        ;   returns ref[Alpha]?  /  return x   (x: ref[Beta])
        ;       -> `ret ptr %0` -- two unrelated concepts confused outright
        ;   returns int          /  return x   (x: ref[Alpha])
        ;       -> `ret i64 0`  -- not even the address, a zero
        ; and the same hole at a typed binding (`let q: ref[Alpha]? x`).
        ;
        ; The acceptor is types_compatible# deliberately, NOT types_equal#:
        ; the looseness it carries is the language's own (integer family,
        ; String -> pointer[const_char], null -> Option, and s96/s170 letting
        ; a concrete pointer[T] stand for ref[T]?). That last one is exactly
        ; the shape that prompted this check and it must keep passing -- what
        ; was wrong was never that `pointer[T]` answered a `ref[T]?`, it was
        ; that nobody asked.
        ;
        ; ONE statement at each call site: an inline branch in the hot
        ; planners perturbs the s178 alloca pool (six -g checks, measured) --
        ; the discipline report_null_into_ref# and report_unknown_identifier#
        ; already follow.
        ;
        ; ★★★ THE CALIBRATION IS THE WHOLE STORY, and the numbers are the
        ; argument: with types_compatible# alone this reported 4683 findings
        ; over the twenty-one package roots, and every reduction came from
        ; READING them rather than counting them. 4683 -> 205 once a region
        ; construction and an Option-into-non-optional were accepted, 205 -> 12
        ; with union variants, pointer[void] and pointee equality, 12 -> 4 with
        ; the four gaps the findings themselves named -- of which three were
        ; statements about THIS FILE, not about the ports: qualified names not
        ; normalised, pointer[void] refusing a reference-shaped source, and a
        ; nested variant construction reporting its innermost payload.
        ; ★ The residue was 3 sites, all genuine, and the precision worth
        ; recording: `return vm.fail()` appears FOURTEEN times in
        ; dazzle/Insn.scaly and exactly the TWO inside a routine returning
        ; something other than ref[Insn]? were reported.
        ; Does this type's HEAD name a concept this root laid out, a
        ; primitive, or one of the language's own wrappers? Anything else is
        ; an unresolved name wearing a type's clothes.
        procedure type_head_is_resolvable(mutable this: Planner, t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked
























        procedure type_conformance_unjudgeable(
            mutable this: Planner,
            target_type: PlannedType,
            source: PlannedOperand
        ) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked






































































        ; Is `t` the untyped pointer `pointer[void]`?
        function type_is_void_pointer(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked















        ; Strip one `Option` wrapper and then one `pointer`/`ref` wrapper off
        ; `target_type`, and ask whether what remains IS `source_type`.
        function type_is_pointee_of(target_type: PlannedType, source_type: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked






























        ; type_conforms_as_variant#: a UNION accepts any of its variants'
        ; payloads directly -- `return JsonNull()` from a `returns JsonValue`
        ; function is the documented construction, not a mismatch. This is the
        ; question lookup_variant_tag# already answers (it keys on the PAYLOAD
        ; TYPE's name, which is what `choose ... when TypeName:` needs), so the
        ; check borrows it rather than re-deriving the union's arm list.
        procedure type_conforms_as_variant(mutable this: Planner, target_type: PlannedType, source_type: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked




        ; ... and NESTED, because a nested variant construction reports the
        ; INNERMOST payload's type. `Expression.Constant(Constant.Integer(
        ; IntegerConstant(...)))` arrives as `IntegerConstant` against a
        ; declared `Expression`, and `IntegerConstant` is a payload of
        ; `Constant`, which is a payload of `Expression` -- two levels down.
        ; The depth is bounded rather than trusted: a union reachable from
        ; itself would otherwise not terminate, and three levels covers every
        ; shape in the tree with one to spare.
        procedure variant_payload_accepts(mutable this: Planner, union_name: String, source_name: String, depth: int) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked










































        ; Do two type names agree once each is reduced to its LAST dotted
        ; segment? `Option[ref[PlannedUnion]]` and
        ; `Option[ref[scalyc.compiler.Plan.PlannedUnion]]` are ONE type spelled
        ; two ways, and types_equal# compares the names whole -- so the
        ; qualified spelling disagrees with itself. Compared byte-wise from
        ; each name's own tail rather than through substring#, which would
        ; allocate a String per comparison.
        function name_tails_equal(a: String, b: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked


























        ; types_equal# modulo package qualification, at every generic level.
        ; Deliberately NOT folded into types_equal# itself: that function is
        ; what overload SELECTION ranks on, so loosening it could change which
        ; function a call picks anywhere in the tree. Here it can only ever
        ; suppress a diagnostic.
        function types_equal_unqualified(a: PlannedType, b: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked































        ; Peel ONE `pointer`/`ref` wrapper off `t` into `out`, answering
        ; whether there was one to peel.
        procedure type_strip_one_reference(t: PlannedType, mutable out: ref[PlannedType]) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked
















        ; Is this type a borrowed/raw reference, in any of its three spellings?
        function type_is_reference_shaped(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked



























        ; The one-byte char family, including `const_char`, which
        ; is_integer_type# does not list. is_char_like_cross_compatible# is the
        ; established question but its `char` arm omits `const_char` while its
        ; `const_char` and `i8` arms both name `char` -- an asymmetry left
        ; alone here on purpose, since that helper feeds types_compatible#'s
        ; pointer-inner rule and widening it would reach overload resolution.
        function type_conformance_char_family(target_type: PlannedType, source_type: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked









        ; type_conformance_accepted#: the acceptor, and it is deliberately
        ; the LOOSE one. types_compatible# alone reported 4683 findings over
        ; the package roots, and reading them rather than counting them showed
        ; two structural classes that are the LANGUAGE, not defects:
        ;
        ;   1. `T where pointer[T]` -- a REGION CONSTRUCTION. `Vector[T]^page(n)`
        ;      yields a pointer while its result_type names the object, so
        ;      `set vector: Vector[T]^own_page(capacity)` looked like a value
        ;      assigned to a pointer slot. region_value_matches_pointer# is the
        ;      question that already exists for exactly this seam (it is what
        ;      init-argument resolution asks), so it is asked here too.
        ;      Biggest single class in the raw run.
        ;   2. `Option[ref[X]] where pointer[X]` -- an Option flowing into a
        ;      NULLABLE target. A `pointer[T]` may hold null by construction
        ;      (that is the whole difference between the two spellings), so
        ;      unwrapping the NPO wrapper onto one loses no claim: both sides
        ;      say the same thing about null. Into a `ref[T]` -- or into any
        ;      value type -- it is REFUSED, because `ref` means non-null:
        ;      report_null_into_ref# already says so about a literal null, and
        ;      an Option is that same null one hop further on. There is no flow
        ;      narrowing here, so a caller that has PROVEN the value non-null
        ;      says so with the explicit `x as ref[X]` unwrap, which is exactly
        ;      what the ARGUMENT position has always demanded --
        ;      types_compatible# has no rule accepting an Option into a plain
        ;      `ref` parameter. Permitting it at the four declared-type
        ;      positions made one language have two answers for one question.
        ;
        ; Both are asked AFTER types_compatible#, so the common answer stays
        ; one call.
        procedure type_conformance_accepted(target_type: PlannedType, mutable source_type: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked






















































































































        ; make_deref_operand#: build the operand the prefix `*` collapse
        ; builds, for a value that is already planned.  Same intrinsic
        ; operator call, same mangled name, same result type -- so a
        ; transparent read emits byte-identically to the `*r` it replaces.
        procedure make_deref_operand(
            mutable this: Planner,
            op: PlannedOperand,
            elem: PlannedType
        ) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedOperand linked





















        ; deref_ref_into_pointee#: a `ref[T]` reaching a context that wants a
        ; `T` IS that value.  Transparent reading is what a borrowed reference
        ; MEANS, and `*r` is the pointer idiom it replaces.
        ;
        ; Nothing loaded here until 2026-08-27 and EVERY read position was
        ; silently wrong, at rc 0: `return r` and `let v: int r` emitted
        ; `ret i64 0`, a call argument passed the ADDRESS as an int (measured
        ; 6090403468), a cast truncated it, and only `*r` answered correctly.
        ; type_conformance_accepted#'s class 8 already ACCEPTS the shape --
        ; indirection is deliberately not its subject -- so the acceptance was
        ; there and only the load was missing.
        ;
        ; The NULLABLE spelling `ref[T]?` is deliberately NOT loaded: that is
        ; the null claim the three ref gates rest on, and such a site has to
        ; spell the `x as ref[T]` unwrap it is relying on.
        ; Does `op` need that load against `target_type`?  The predicate is
        ; separate so a call site that must know WHETHER it converted can ask
        ; -- the same split `operand_retypes_to_integer` /
        ; `retype_integer_operand` uses.
        procedure operand_derefs_to_pointee(mutable target_type: PlannedType, mutable op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked































































        ; address_of_is_identity#: does `&x` hand back the operand ITSELF?
        ;
        ; MEASURED against the emitter, shape by shape. `&` yields the address of
        ; a SLOT where there is one and the operand's own value where there is
        ; not:
        ;   a ref/pointer PARAMETER, a `let`-bound reference   `ret %0`   identity
        ;   `var r: ref[X] b` / `ref[X]? null` / `pointer[X] null`  `ret %r`  the cell
        ;   a stack-struct var, a heap-bound var               the object    wrap
        ;   a member access `&r.field`                         a GEP        wrap
        ; The identity case is therefore exactly a BARE reference-shaped variable
        ; that is not mutable -- one with no slot of its own to address.
        ;
        ; ★★★ Before this the planner wrapped unconditionally, so `&b` on a
        ; `ref[Box]` was typed `ref[ref[Box]]` while the emitter handed back `b`.
        ; type_conformance_accepted#'s class 8 exists ONLY to accept that lie,
        ; and every rule that counts indirection had to stay lenient by exactly
        ; the level it was counting in order to survive it -- which is how
        ; operand_derefs_to_pointee# came to skip a load it owed
        ; (ref_cell_nonoptional.scaly). **A rule that has to be inexact to
        ; tolerate a wrong type is the wrong type's cost, paid somewhere else.**
        function address_of_is_identity(op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked
















        ; set_target_stores_through#: `set r: v` on a `ref[T]` target.
        ;
        ; A borrowed reference cannot be rebound through a name it does not
        ; own, so the useful reading is the C++ one -- the assignment writes
        ; the POINTEE.  Until 2026-08-27 it wrote nothing at all: the target is
        ; not `is_mutable` (a parameter never is), the Variable arm returned
        ; early, and the statement vanished at rc 0 with no diagnostic.
        ;
        ; The RHS decides, which is the only rule that can serve both: a value
        ; of the pointee type stores THROUGH, a reference of the target's own
        ; type REBINDS (a nullable local's ordinary move).  Judged with the
        ; conformance relation, so every shape it already accepts -- a
        ; construction standing for a reference, an erased instantiation --
        ; behaves the same here.
        ; The pointee of a `ref[T]`/`pointer[T]`, or the type itself when
        ; there is nothing to peel.
        procedure strip_one_reference_or_self(mutable t: PlannedType) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true returns PlannedType linked







        ; Is this operand a bare MUTABLE variable, i.e. a `var` local?  A
        ; parameter and a `let` are not, and that is what decides an ambiguous
        ; `set` -- see set_target_stores_through#.
        ; operand_is_rebindable_target#: may a `set` on this target put a REFERENCE
        ; into it rather than store through it? A mutable variable can be rebound,
        ; and so can the SLOT a pointer dereference names: `set *(p + i): r` with
        ; `p: pointer[ref[X]]` writes the reference into the buffer. Answering
        ; false there made set_target_stores_through# read the element store of
        ; every generic container instantiated on a non-optional reference --
        ; `Array[ref[X]].add`, `Vector[ref[X]].put` -- as a copy THROUGH the
        ; still uninitialized slot: `load ptr` + `store %X`, SIGSEGV on the first
        ; `add` (2026-09-14, tscaly's semver range; regress
        ; array_of_ref_elements).
        ; ★ A dereference of a POINTER only. `*r` on a non-optional `ref[T]` is
        ; refused by report_deref_of_ref#, and `*opt` on a `ref[T]?` names the
        ; referent, which is not a slot the statement may re-point.
        function operand_is_rebindable_target(op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked































        function operand_is_mutable_variable(op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked










        ; ref_pointee_may_be_null#: is the POINTEE of this `ref[T]` itself a
        ; slot that may hold nothing?
        ;
        ; The question BOTH null gates have to ask before they judge a
        ; reference, and the one neither asked.  A `ref[ref[X]?]` -- the
        ; pointer-pointee out-cell -- is a reference that always refers to
        ; something, and what it refers to is an Option: `set r: null` writes
        ; the pointee and `r = null` reads it, so neither statement is the lie
        ; the gates were built to catch.  Judging the reference refused both
        ; spellings, while `*r` is refused by report_deref_of_ref#, and the
        ; tree was left with no way to say either thing.
        ;
        ; ★★★ IT SERVES THE WRITE SIDE ONLY, and that was measured rather than
        ; reasoned.  `set r: null` reaches the pointee because
        ; set_target_stores_through# rewrites the target into the deref; a
        ; comparison `r = null` does NOT -- it takes the NPO presence-test path
        ; and never loads -- so report_null_test_on_ref# keeps its verdict and
        ; says so in as many words.  The first draft exempted both and turned a
        ; true diagnostic into a constant comparison.
        ;
        ; ★ `pointer` counts as well as `Option`: a pointer pointee is nullable
        ; by construction, which is the same reason report_null_into_ref#
        ; leaves a `pointer[T]` TARGET alone.
        procedure ref_pointee_may_be_null(mutable t: PlannedType) @ret_param_0 true returns bool linked











        ; null_set_target#: which type report_null_into_ref# must judge for a
        ; `set` whose source is the bare null literal.
        ;
        ; `set r: null` on a `ref[T]` has exactly ONE reading -- a borrowed
        ; reference cannot be rebound to nothing, so the assignment writes the
        ; POINTEE, which is set_target_stores_through#'s rule for every other
        ; source.  When that pointee is itself nullable the statement is
        ; correct and the gate must judge the POINTEE, not the reference
        ; around it.
        ;
        ; ★★★ It was found by CONVERTING, not by reading: the pointer-pointee
        ; out-cell `out: ref[ref[X]?]` -- the route CLAUDE.md records as proven
        ; and never executed -- opens with `set out: null` at nearly every one
        ; of its sites, and the tree had NO spelling left for it.  Judging the
        ; reference refused the honest form, while `set *out: null` is refused
        ; by report_deref_of_ref#, so the two gates closed on each other and
        ; the only way through was a scratch local (`var nothing: ref[X]? null`
        ; then `set out: nothing`) -- a workaround for a statement the language
        ; should simply accept.
        ;
        ; A pointee that is NOT nullable keeps the reference's own verdict:
        ; storing null through into a non-optional pointee is the same lie one
        ; level in, and `ref[int]` must still refuse it.
        ;
        ; ★ A BARE target only, for set_target_stores_through#'s reason -- a
        ; member-access chain names the REFERENCE, not the pointee -- and the
        ; two must agree, or the gate would fall silent on a statement the
        ; rewrite does not take.
        procedure null_set_target(mutable target: PlannedOperand) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true returns PlannedType linked











        procedure set_target_stores_through(mutable this: Planner, mutable target_type: PlannedType, mutable source_type: PlannedType, target_is_mutable: bool) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked




























































        ; report_unassignable_ref_target#: a `set` whose target is a
        ; NON-MUTABLE reference and whose source stores through nothing.
        ;
        ; The emitter's Variable arm returns without storing when the target is
        ; not `is_mutable`, so such a statement is a SILENT no-op -- which is
        ; how three `store %Entity` vanished out of opensp's
        ; parse_external_entity when a variant payload was not recognised as
        ; conforming to its union (the corpus went 380/380 to 287/380, every
        ; failure an entity or a subdoc). The store-through rewrite is what
        ; normally saves such a statement; when it does not fire, saying so is
        ; the only thing between the author and a dropped assignment.
        procedure report_unassignable_ref_target(
            mutable this: Planner,
            target: PlannedOperand,
            loc: Span
        ) linked





































        procedure deref_ref_into_pointee(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable target_type: PlannedType,
            mutable op: PlannedOperand
        ) @page true @direct true returns PlannedOperand linked









        ; report_integer_narrowing#: a WRITE into a narrower integer is spelled
        ; with its cast. types_compatible#'s Rule 2 accepts every integer for
        ; every other, which is right for READING (the emitter widens, and since
        ; 2026-09-17 a typed binding and a right operand widen by their own
        ; signedness) and silent for WRITING: `set n.pos: offset` into an i32
        ; field kept the low half of an int at rc 0. Decided 2026-09-17 with the
        ; author: reads widen implicitly, a narrowing store says `as i32`.
        ;
        ; Judged at the typed binding and the `set`, the two positions a write
        ; takes; a bare literal that FITS is exempt because it is retyped to the
        ; target (operand_retypes_to_integer#), an explicit `as` is exempt
        ; because its result already has the target's width.
        procedure report_integer_narrowing(
            mutable this: Planner,
            target_type: PlannedType,
            source: PlannedOperand,
            loc: Span,
            position: String
        ) linked































        procedure report_type_mismatch(
            mutable this: Planner,
            target_type: PlannedType,
            mutable source: PlannedOperand,
            loc: Span,
            position: String
        ) linked

























        ; report_ref_arithmetic#: `&x` yields ref[T], and arithmetic on a
        ; BORROWED REFERENCE is not a reference operation -- it is pointer
        ; arithmetic, which is what `pointer[T]` is for. Without this the
        ; operand falls past the pointer-arith fallback with a fresh type var,
        ; and emit_intrinsic_binary_op's GEP then uses BYTE stride: measured,
        ; `&b + 1` on a two-field struct stepped 1 byte instead of 16, with no
        ; diagnostic anywhere. That silent miscompile is the whole reason this
        ; check has to land in the SAME commit as `&`-yields-ref. The exit is a
        ; deliberate cast -- `(&b as pointer[Box]) + 1` -- which restores the
        ; element stride, so the twelve hand-rolled buffers in opensp/dazzle
        ; need one line each and the language needs no exception rule.
        ; ONE statement at the call site: an inline branch in
        ; collapse_operand_sequence# perturbs its s178 alloca pool (six -g
        ; checks, measured).
        ; Is this planned type an `Option` whose sole generic argument is a
        ; `ref`, i.e. the NPO spelling `ref[T]?`? The Emitter asks the same
        ; question as `is_npo_option`; this is the Planner-side twin over a
        ; PlannedType. A `pointer[T]?` is deliberately NOT included: a pointer
        ; carries its element stride and arithmetic on it is legal.
        ; subscript_receiver_type#: peel the borrow layers off a subscript
        ; receiver so `a[i]` finds the CONTAINER's `operator []`.
        ; find_member_operator# auto-derefs `pointer` and `ref` -- BY NAME, and
        ; the s113 NPO wrapper is named `Option`, so on the ordinary port field
        ; shape `ref[Array[T]]?` the lookup asked the Option concept, found no
        ; `[]`, and the pre-pass left the pair unfused: the operand stood alone
        ; and `[i]` became an orphan ("unexpected operand - missing operator").
        ; The same receiver answers `*(a.get_buffer() + i)` fine, which is what
        ; made it look like a subscript problem rather than a NAME test.
        ; ★Deliberately NOT fixed inside find_member_operator#: that helper also
        ; serves the binary member operators, where unwrapping an Option would
        ; let `x <> null` on a `ref[X]?` dispatch to X's own `==` instead of the
        ; language's NPO presence test. Narrow to the `[]` site, which is the
        ; one that asked.
        ; ★Peels at most four layers, and only NPO Options -- a REAL Option (a
        ; tagged union over a non-pointer payload) is a value, not a borrow.
        function subscript_receiver_type(t: PlannedType) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true returns PlannedType linked




























        ; Is this the NPO spelling of a BORROW -- `ref[T]?` or `pointer[T]?`,
        ; i.e. an Option whose sole generic argument is itself pointer-shaped?
        ; planned_type_is_npo_ref# above answers only for `ref`, because the
        ; arithmetic gate it serves must let a pointer through.
        ; report_subscript_assignment#: `set a[i]: v` on a CONTAINER is not a
        ; store. `operator []` answers the element BY VALUE (2026-08-27), so
        ; there is no slot behind `a[i]`, and the emitter's Call target arm has
        ; no arm for a `[]` call: the whole statement was DROPPED, rc 0, with
        ; nothing said at the line that caused it. The write is `put`.
        ; ★★★It cost FOUR live miscompiles that stood for a day.
        ; `tools/subscript.py` rewrote four `set` TARGETS from
        ; `set *(x.get_buffer() + i): v` into `set x[i]: v` (commit 25614a02,
        ; "74 buffer walks become subscripts"): dazzle's `#!optional`/`#!key`
        ; DEFAULTS were then never stored, so every default read back as `#f`,
        ; and the root-rule specificity sort never swapped a pair. Five backend
        ; suites plus `tests/dazzle/engine` went red, and the engine red was
        ; written down in CLAUDE.md as "not ours".
        ; ★A POINTER subscript is untouched and must stay so: the pre-pass
        ; FUSES `p[i]` into the `*` deref call, which is a real store and is
        ; what every hand-rolled buffer in the tree writes through. Only a `[]`
        ; CALL -- a container's own operator -- ever reaches here.
        ; ★The judgement is the RESULT SHAPE, not the receiver. A subscript
        ; that answers `pointer[T]`/`ref[T]` NAMES a slot, and a store through
        ; one is the accessor-store the emitter's ISet arm already serves; only
        ; a by-value answer has nowhere to put the value.
        procedure report_subscript_assignment(mutable this: Planner, target: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true linked




















        ; planned_type_is_slot#: does this planned type NAME a location a store
        ; can land in? The three borrow spellings do -- `pointer[T]`, `ref[T]`
        ; and the s113 NPO `Option` over either -- and nothing else does.
        ; ★Ask the SHAPE, not the name: the NPO wrapper is named `Option`, and
        ; a test that only compares against "pointer"/"ref" is this compiler's
        ; standing bug pattern.
        function planned_type_is_slot(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked








        function planned_type_is_npo_borrow(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked

















        function planned_type_is_npo_ref(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked














        procedure report_ref_arithmetic(
            mutable this: Planner,
            lhs_type: PlannedType,
            rhs_type: PlannedType,
            op_name: String,
            loc: Span
        ) linked












































        ; report_deref_of_non_pointer#: `*x` on a SCALAR. Until 2026-08-27 it
        ; emitted `load i64, i64 %0` -- INVALID IR, whose pointer operand is an
        ; integer -- at rc 0 with no word, and the program then died with
        ; SIGSEGV wherever that integer happened to point. It stayed invisible
        ; because nothing ever asked: get_pointer_element_type answers null for
        ; a non-pointer and the prefix arm simply kept the operand's own type.
        ; The check earns its place with the container-subscript change of the
        ; same day: `operator []` answers the ELEMENT BY VALUE now, so every
        ; stale `*a[i]` is exactly this shape -- without a diagnostic that
        ; migration would be a silent crash instead of a compile error, which
        ; is the opposite of what a breaking change owes its callers.
        ; NARROW ON PURPOSE: only a PRIMITIVE SCALAR is judged. A struct deref
        ; is just as wrong, but a cross-package placeholder arrives as an
        ; opaque struct that IS a pointer, and an unresolved type variable must
        ; not be guessed at. Report what is proven, not what is suspected.
        function type_is_underefable_scalar(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked


















        procedure report_deref_of_non_pointer(
            mutable this: Planner,
            operand_type: PlannedType,
            loc: Span
        ) linked














        ; report_deref_of_ref#: THE FIFTH GATE on a non-optional `ref[T]`, and
        ; the one the other four were building towards. A `ref[T]` reads and
        ; writes as its pointee (deref_ref_into_pointee#), so `*r` is the
        ; POINTER idiom on a value that is not a pointer -- the same line
        ; report_ref_arithmetic# draws for `+`.
        ;
        ; ★It could only land AFTER the transparent readings existed and after
        ; the tree was swept, because until then `*r` was the ONLY spelling
        ; that worked: 666 stores, 26 reads and 101 deref receivers had to move
        ; first, each verified by BYTE-IDENTICAL emission of every root (the
        ; deref sweep) or by the gates (the receivers, where a whole-union
        ; memcpy goes away).
        ;
        ; ★★★The NULLABLE spelling `ref[T]?` is NOT judged, and that is a
        ; statement about the language rather than a gap: `ref[T]?` is
        ; `Option[ref[T]]`, no transparent reading exists for an Option -- the
        ; three null gates rest on an Option having to spell its unwrap -- and
        ; `*opt` remains the spelling at several hundred sites. The gate asks
        ; the NAME here on purpose, which is the one place in this family where
        ; that is right: `Option` is exactly what must NOT be caught.
        procedure report_deref_of_ref(
            mutable this: Planner,
            operand_type: PlannedType,
            loc: Span
        ) linked













        ; report_null_test_on_ref#: the third gate on a NON-OPTIONAL `ref[T]`,
        ; and the one that makes the other two enforceable in the ports.
        ; `report_ref_arithmetic#` forbids `r + 1`; `report_null_into_ref#`
        ; forbids null flowing IN at the four positions where a target type is
        ; in hand. Neither can see the position where the lie is actually
        ; VISIBLE: a body that tests its own non-optional reference against
        ; null. Under the declaration that comparison is constant -- dead code
        ; that reads as a live guard -- so it is never anything but a wrong
        ; declaration.
        ;
        ; ★★★Why a checker and not another sweep. The 2026-08-23 conversion
        ; (a72ed732, "Declare concept refs as ref in the ports") added 4081
        ; `: ref[...]` declarations across opensp and dazzle and NOT ONE
        ; `ref[...]?` -- it was a flat `pointer[X]` -> `ref[X]` rewrite with no
        ; nullability analysis, and `tools/refconv.py`, which has that rule,
        ; did not exist yet. By the time it did, those sites no longer carried
        ; the string `pointer[` that is its only trigger, so the tool can never
        ; revisit them: it is ONE-WAY. Measured 2026-08-26 -- 241 provable
        ; sites (202 parameters, 20 locals, 19 fields), all of them in exactly
        ; the two packages that commit touched, and ZERO in the five that
        ; refconv itself converted. A lexical sweep would leave the class
        ; reachable again by the next blind rewrite; a planner gate cannot be
        ; regressed by any tool.
        ;
        ; It reports the PROVABLE class only. A parameter that merely FORWARDS
        ; its value to a nullable parameter of another routine is nullable too
        ; and tests nothing, so nothing here or in any tool can see it; those
        ; stay unaudited on purpose rather than being guessed at.
        ;
        ; The name test is the right one and for the same reason it is right in
        ; report_null_into_ref#: the nullable spelling arrives wrapped as
        ; `Option` (Modeler s113), so asking for "ref" skips exactly the form
        ; that IS the fix. `pointer[T]` is skipped for a different reason -- a
        ; pointer may always be null, by design, and `pointer[T]?` likewise.
        ; ONE statement at the call site: an inline branch in
        ; collapse_operand_sequence# perturbs its s178 alloca pool (six -g
        ; checks, measured).
        procedure report_null_test_on_ref(
            mutable this: Planner,
            lhs: PlannedOperand,
            rhs: PlannedOperand,
            op_name: String,
            loc: Span
        ) linked





























































        procedure report_receiver_arity(
            mutable this: Planner,
            is_dispatch: bool,
            match_ref: ref[MethodMatch]?,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            fn_name: String,
            loc: Span
        ) linked



















































        ; gate_char_like: the one-byte family as a CALL BOUNDARY sees it.
        ; type_is_char_like# omits const_char deliberately — it decides whether
        ; a bare char LITERAL may fold — so report_method_arg_types# asks its
        ; own question rather than widening that one.
        function gate_char_like(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked






        ; gate_strip_indirection: peel Option / ref / pointer wrappers off a
        ; type until a plain one is reached (bounded, so a cyclic or absurdly
        ; nested type cannot spin). ONLY for report_method_arg_types# — it is
        ; deliberately NOT types_compatible#, which overload SELECTION depends
        ; on: tightening or loosening that function moves which candidate is
        ; chosen, i.e. emission, everywhere.
        ;
        ; The gate needs the looser question because types_compatible answers
        ; NO for two shapes this language passes freely, both measured on the
        ; compiler's own sources: an NPO Option wrapper on either side
        ; (`plan_function(name, t)` where the parameter is declared
        ; `ref[PlannedType]?`, which s113 wraps to Option[ref[PlannedType]] —
        ; the seam CLAUDE.md records hiding a pointer-shaped thing under a name
        ; no name-test knows), and an Option flowing INTO a plain parameter
        ; (`Emitter.emit`, argument Option[ref[Plan]] against `Plan`), which the
        ; nullability rule explicitly allows. Rules 3/4 cover ONE direction of
        ; the pointer/ref deref only, so a value handed to a `ref[T]` parameter
        ; is rejected too.
        ;
        ; Consequence, stated rather than hidden: an INDIRECTION mistake is not
        ; reported by this gate — pointer[A] and A agree here. What survives is
        ; the class it was built for, a genuinely different type.
        ;
        ; Single exit: a by-value struct returned from several branches
        ; region-allocates per site and the merging phi fails llc verification.
        function gate_strip_indirection(t: PlannedType) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true returns PlannedType linked





































        ; report_method_arg_types: the TYPE half of lookup_method#'s call gate
        ; (2026-08-23). Its sibling arity gate has counted a method call's
        ; arguments since 2026-08-01; their TYPES were compared only when a
        ; concept declared SEVERAL same-named methods, because the s163
        ; selection loop — which owns method_params_match#, the one place a
        ; declared parameter type ever meets an argument type — is gated on
        ; `candidates.length > 1`. A SINGLE by-name candidate was therefore
        ; taken unexamined: handing it an argument of the wrong type planned
        ; clean with rc 0, and emit_call builds the callee's type from the
        ; ARGUMENT VALUES rather than from the declaration, so llc had nothing
        ; to object to either — the callee read whatever the caller happened to
        ; leave in the register. Same class as the receiver-arity and
        ; static-arity gates, and visible from the same side: a FREE function
        ; with the identical mistake is a hard `function not found`, because
        ; check_function_match# always compares.
        ;
        ; ★★★THREE restrictions, each measured against the stdlib rather than
        ; guessed — without them the gate reports 26 findings there, all its
        ; own imprecision:
        ;
        ; (a) Only a method that declares `this`. lookup_method# also serves a
        ;     this-LESS member reached through a value receiver, whose receiver
        ;     fills the first DECLARED parameter (`old.is_equal(gid)`,
        ;     dazzle/ELObj.scaly), so its arg_types sit one slot off against
        ;     f.input and every comparison would be against the wrong
        ;     parameter. Exactly the restriction the arity gate's exact arm
        ;     carries, for the same reason.
        ; (b) Only a NON-GENERIC receiver. Binding the receiver's parameters
        ;     the way the selection loop does is not sound here: 
        ;     define_type_substitution appends and lookup is FIRST-WINS, so an
        ;     AMBIENT binding of the same letter wins over the one just made.
        ;     Measured: `slot_list.add(own_page, Slot[T](…))` on a
        ;     List[Slot[T]] inside HashSetBuilder[T] planned its
        ;     `element: T` as the ambient T (String) instead of the receiver's
        ;     Slot[String], and the gate reported a correct call. The same
        ;     trap sits in the selection loop, where it can only mis-RANK
        ;     overloads; here it would reject working code, so the whole class
        ;     stays out until substitution scoping is fixed.
        ; (c) Per argument, anything neither side has resolved is skipped: an
        ;     argument type that is a fresh variable or has no name at all
        ;     (measured 20× on Vector.get_ptr), and a parameter that still
        ;     mentions a generic parameter — a generic METHOD of a plain
        ;     concept — since an unbound var compares as matching anything and
        ;     the check would be vacuous rather than wrong.
        ;
        ; What is left is the shape the bug was found in: a plain concept, a
        ; concrete signature, one candidate. The comparison is the PERMISSIVE
        ; pass — types_compatible plus the char and float folds — i.e. exactly
        ; what the selection's last pass accepts, so nothing reported here
        ; could have been selected by it either.
        ;
        ; Record and CONTINUE, like the arity gate: add_diagnostic is fatal at
        ; the cli.scaly gate, so resolution proceeds exactly as it did before.
        procedure report_method_arg_types(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable f: Function,
            lookup_st: PlannedType,
            method_name: String,
            arg_types: Vector[PlannedType],
            arg_ops: Vector[PlannedOperand],
            loc: Span
        ) io linked
















































































































































        ; report_variant_arg_arity: a union ARM carries at most ONE payload, so
        ; a variant construction takes at most one argument — and until
        ; 2026-08-18 both planner paths that build one took component 0 and
        ; dropped the rest WORDLESS, rc 0 (`U.First(1, 2)` answered 1). The two
        ; call sites are the resolved gate (is_variant_ctor) and the s185 rescue
        ; for a bare `Some(5)`; both call this in ONE statement, because an
        ; inline branch in collapse_operand_sequence# perturbs its s178 alloca
        ; pool — the same discipline report_unknown_identifier# follows.
        ;
        ; A payload record with several fields is constructed on its own
        ; (`U.First(Rec(11, 22, 33))`); spelling its fields into the variant
        ; construction is what this names.
        procedure report_variant_arg_arity(
            mutable this: Planner,
            union_name: String,
            variant_name: String,
            provided_count: size_t,
            loc: Span
        ) linked

















        ; report_variant_payload_shape#: the payload of a variant construction must
        ; have the REFERENCE SHAPE its arm declares. Neither planner path compared
        ; the argument's type with the arm's, and the emitter stores whatever it is
        ; handed, so both directions compiled at rc 0 (measured 2026-09-17): a VALUE
        ; into a `ref[T]` arm (`U.B(Big(1, 2, 3, 4))`, `U.B(big)`, and a `Big^page(…)`
        ; without the `&`) SIGSEGVs on the first read through the binding, and a
        ; REFERENCE into a by-value arm reads its address back as the record's
        ; fields. tscaly met the first shape boxing NodeData's large arms and
        ; checked 254 construction sites by a scan because the compiler would not.
        ;
        ; Only the SHAPE is judged, never the pointee: the tuple path's type check
        ; (report_component_type_mismatch#) carries the measured exemptions for
        ; the rest. A GENERIC union is not judged either — its arm types name
        ; parameters this site does not bind (Option[T].Some is the NPO case).
        ; One statement per call site, as report_variant_arg_arity# above.
        procedure report_variant_payload_shape(
            mutable this: Planner,
            mutable union_name: String,
            variant_name: String,
            payload: ref[PlannedOperand]?,
            loc: Span
        ) io linked














































        ; ★★★ A METHOD CALL WHOSE MEMBER DOES NOT EXIST WAS NOT A CALL AT ALL,
        ; AND THAT IS WHY IT VANISHED. Call detection above accepts exactly two
        ; shapes -- a member_access chain whose LAST HOP is_method, or a
        ; PlannedType head -- and an unresolved hop is neither: resolve_member_
        ; access# records a placeholder with is_method=false and a VOID result
        ; type, so `is_call` stays false, the operand pair collapses, and the
        ; statement leaves no trace. rc 0, no diagnostic, not even a dropped-
        ; call message, because the s185 not-found gate lives INSIDE `if
        ; is_call` and was never reached.
        ;
        ; ★★★The receiver's type decided whether ANYTHING was said, and only by
        ; accident: for a CONCEPT the emitter later tripped over the dangling
        ; member READ (`Emitter: member not found`, exit 16, no file and no
        ; line), while for a PRIMITIVE pointee -- `q.put(0, c)` on a
        ; `q: pointer[char]`, which derefs to `char` -- map_type answers `i8`,
        ; the emitter's struct-shaped trap does not fire, and the whole body
        ; came out `ret void`. Measured 2026-09-03: tscaly's
        ; get_property_name_for_known_symbol_name wrote its fallback name with
        ; three `buf.put(w, ...)` into a `buf: pointer[char]` and had emitted
        ; NOTHING since the first port.
        ;
        ; ★The gate asks for the placeholder EXACTLY: last hop not a method,
        ; void result, and a NON-EMPTY parent name. The empty name is the one
        ; legitimate recovery the placeholder arm documents (a base that itself
        ; did not resolve, already reported at its own line); a resolved
        ; property hop carries its own type, so a delegate-typed field called
        ; through `obj.f(args)` is untouched.
        procedure report_unresolved_method_call(
            mutable this: Planner,
            is_call: bool,
            recv: PlannedOperand,
            second: PlannedOperand
        ) linked


















































        procedure report_unknown_identifier(mutable this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true linked




















        ; collapse_grouping_target#: a PARENTHESIZED `set` target is a
        ; GROUPING, not a value. `set (p).v: 55` means what `set p.v: 55`
        ; means and `set (*p).v: 77` means the store the author wrote -- but
        ; the emitter's target dispatch has arms for Variable / Call /
        ; GlobalRef only, so a Tuple target fell into its silent `else` and
        ; the WHOLE statement was dropped: rc 0, no diagnostic, no store in
        ; the emitted body at all (measured -- the `set` is simply absent
        ; from `main`).
        ;
        ; In VALUE position the same grouping is CORRECT and that asymmetry
        ; is the whole defect: emit_tuple's s160 arm loads the struct out of
        ; `(*p)`, so `let x (*p).v` READS the right field while
        ; `set (*p).v: 77` writes into that loaded COPY. Reading works,
        ; writing evaporates.
        ;
        ; Unwrap a single ANONYMOUS component holding a single operand and
        ; hand the inner expression on with the two member-access chains
        ; CONCATENATED, so the store travels the same path `set p.v:` and
        ; `set accessor(i).f:` already travel. A NAMED component is a real
        ; construction, never a grouping, and is left alone.
        ;
        ; This also puts the target in front of the checks below that choose
        ; on its expr -- the unknown-assignment-target gate, the integer /
        ; float / cstr retypes, and the frame-external STORE analysis, all of
        ; which a Tuple slipped past.
        function collapse_grouping_target(this: Planner, operand: PlannedOperand) @page true @direct true returns PlannedOperand linked

























































































        procedure plan_action(mutable this: Planner, mutable a: Action) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns PlannedAction io linked

















































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































































        ; Escape checker (RBMM blocker C): true for two escaping shapes:
        ;  (1) a ref[T]/pointer[T] whose POINTEE lives on a Local ($) page — the
        ;      `&` operator and resolve_type keep the pointee (with its life) in
        ;      generics[0];
        ;  (2) a VALUE whose OWN life is Local (design-B item 3) — String()/etc
        ;      carry an internal `data` pointer into the local page, so returning
        ;      the value by copy still hands out a dangling internal pointer.
        ; A Local pointee/value dies at scope exit, so returning/storing it is a
        ; use-after-free. Call (#) and Reference (^name) lifetimes outlive the
        ; function and are fine. Mirrors Planner.cpp::isReferenceToLocalPage.
        ; True when the operand IS a freshly-built page-owning construction
        ; whose allocation lives on the function's LOCAL page (Local life) or
        ; would (Unspecified life on a page-owning value). is_reference_to_local_
        ; page only inspects result_type.life, but the construction blocks
        ; (init# / reshape) stamp the Local life onto the PlannedCall/PlannedTuple
        ; EXPRESSION (call.life), leaving result_type.life Unspecified — the
        ; emitter selects the page from call.life, not result_type.life. So a
        ; directly-returned no-sigil construction (`return Vector[T](0)`) is a
        ; local-page allocation that is_reference_to_local_page misses. R2 uses
        ; this as an additional gate so such returns are promoted to rp by
        ; restamp_operand_life# (which re-checks the expression life itself).
        ; result_owns_page_guts excludes borrows/handles. Read the EXPRESSION
        ; life, not result_type.life, by design.
        ; WP7: true when `op` is an Unspecified-life value construction
        ; (PlannedCall/PlannedTuple) whose result_type is NOT itself a
        ; pointer/ref — the shape returned as pointer[T] that must be promoted
        ; to the caller page. A call already returning a pointer (`return
        ; create_node(x)`) has a pointer result_type and is excluded, so it is
        ; forwarded unchanged (no false-positive promotion/diagnostic).
        function tail_is_unspecified_value_ctor(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked





























        function operand_is_local_page_construction(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked












































        ; binding_is_frame_local: true when `name` resolves to a non-parameter
        ; local binding whose stored VALUE lives in this frame (life Unspecified
        ; = plain stack value / no-sigil; or Local = $/heap-backed-default). Such
        ; a binding's STORAGE is the frame, so `&name` is a pointer into the
        ; local page (region rank Local). Parameters are excluded: a by-pointer
        ; struct param points at the caller's object (rank Caller), and a value
        ; param copied into the frame is not address-escaped in practice
        ; (pos_return_param_ptr returns the pointer VALUE, never `&param`).
        ; Bindings allocated with #/^ carry Call/Reference life on their VALUE —
        ; the programmer placed them on a caller/named page deliberately — so
        ; `&` of those is left to a later step (their slot is frame, but value
        ; uses are the common case and stay correct). Escape soundness step 1.
        function binding_is_frame_local(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked















        ; base_is_value_struct: true when the local binding `name` stores a
        ; VALUE struct in the frame — NOT a pointer/ref. Escape soundness
        ; step 2: a frame-local pointer var (`var old_current current`,
        ; `let p = ...`) has `.field` AUTO-DEREF to the pointed-to object,
        ; which lives on a caller/heap page — `&p.field` is then a Caller
        ; pointer, NOT a frame address. parent_type can't tell us this
        ; (s173 records the UNWRAPPED type), so the base binding's stored
        ; type is checked directly. Every compiler `return &base.field`
        ; (HashMap/List iterators) has a pointer/ref/^rp base, so this
        ; excludes them all.
        function base_is_value_struct(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked



























        ; option_inner_is_reference: true when `t` is an Option whose first
        ; generic argument is a `pointer`/`ref` — the NPO auto-deref shape.
        function option_inner_is_reference(this: Planner, t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked




















        ; member_chain_all_fields: true when every member-access hop is a
        ; plain field (or union-value) access — no method call. `&x.m()`
        ; addresses a call result, not a frame field, so it is not flagged
        ; here (rare; deferred to step 3). A null chain is vacuously true.
        function member_chain_all_fields(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked




















        ; construction_life_is_frame: true when a construction (PlannedCall
        ; ctor / Tuple / VariantConstruction) lives in THIS frame — i.e. its
        ; life is Unspecified (no sigil) or Local ($). A #/^ construction is
        ; allocated on a caller/named page, so its address is Caller, not
        ; Local. (this is ignored — kept a method to match the call style.)
        function construction_life_is_frame(this: Planner, life: Lifetime) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked












        ; True when `&op` produces a pointer into THIS frame — generalizes
        ; the step-1 bare `&x` to field chains and address-of-temporary.
        ; Kept as its own single-exit helper (not inlined into the prefix-`&`
        ; collapse block): a nested choose/if there is miscompiled by the
        ; bootstrap lineage (the documented braceless-nest trap). Cases:
        ;   (1) bare `&x`            — x a frame-local of ANY type (the slot
        ;                              itself is in the frame).
        ;   (2) `&x.field-chain`     — x a frame-local VALUE struct, all hops
        ;                              plain fields (base_is_value_struct +
        ;                              member_chain_all_fields exclude every
        ;                              compiler pointer/ref-base `&p.field`).
        ;   (3) `&Box(5)` / `&(a,b)` — address of a frame TEMPORARY (no-sigil
        ;                              or $ construction; #/^ live on a page).
        ; `&<^rp-local>` (the slot is frame but the value is Caller) is NOT
        ; flagged — binding_is_frame_local's {Unspecified,Local} gate excludes
        ; Reference life; deferred to a later soundness step.
        function operand_addr_is_frame_local(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked













































        function is_reference_to_local_page(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked




































































        ; region_of_operand#: the single provenance chokepoint (Stage B). Returns
        ; the region rank of the operand's pointee — TODAY a coarse {Local,Caller}
        ; derived from the operand's result_type Local stamp (which already
        ; encodes the step-1/2/3 address-of/deref/field/join provenance walk via
        ; make_pointer_to_local#/make_ref_to_local_inner# + natural type flow).
        ; The 4 bespoke escape sites route their reference-escape gate through
        ; here so phase-5 (Stage D) refines the rank — ordered numeric ages from
        ; signatures, field/inter-proc provenance — in ONE place instead of four.
        ; NEUTRAL: region_of_operand(op) < required_region_for_escape() is
        ; exactly today's is_reference_to_local_page(op.result_type) (rank Local
        ; iff a frame pointee, else Caller; Caller < Static unused yet).
        ; operand_is_stack_closure: true when `op` is a bare read (no member-
        ; access hop) of a local bound to a stack (Lifetime.Local) closure —
        ; recorded in local_closure_names at plan_binding#. A member hop
        ; (`clo.field`) is a read THROUGH the closure, not the closure value, so
        ; it does not qualify. Used by the CLOSURE store-escape check.
        function operand_is_stack_closure(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked















        ; target_is_frame_external: true when a store TARGET writes into memory
        ; that outlives this frame — a parameter/receiver's field
        ; (`this.field`/`param.field`, base binding is_parameter), a module
        ; global (GlobalRef), or through a pointer deref (Call). A same-frame
        ; local's field (base var/let) dies with the frame → false. Mirrors the
        ; inline frame-external detection in the ref STORE vector above.
        function target_is_frame_external(this: Planner, target: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked




























        function region_of_operand(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns size_t linked













































































































        ; required_region_for_escape#: the minimum pointee rank an escape sink
        ; (return / store into Caller-lived memory / pass to a storing callee)
        ; demands. TODAY constant Caller — anything Local-ranked escapes. Stage D
        ; refines this per destination (the returned-into caller region, the
        ; stored-into param's age, the callee's stored-dest age).
        function required_region_for_escape() @ret_nonlocal true returns size_t linked


        ; is_local_ref_pointer: the REFERENCE half of is_reference_to_local_
        ; page# — true ONLY when `t` is a pointer/ref whose inner pointee has
        ; Local life (a genuine `&<frame-local>` reference). It deliberately
        ; OMITS the leading `t.life==Local` clause, which also matches a Local-
        ; life VALUE type (e.g. a type-metadata PlannedType returned by copy
        ; into sret — find_break_type_in_*). Used by plan_binding#'s step-3
        ; gap-1 exclusion: a binding holding such a reference is not a
        ; relocatable allocation and must not be promoted, but a Local-life
        ; value binding stays promotable (returned by copy, no dangle).
        function is_local_ref_pointer(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked




















































        ; result_type_is_value_struct: true when `t` is a by-VALUE struct held
        ; in the frame — not a pointer/ref, and not an Option whose inner is a
        ; reference (NPO auto-deref). The type-based twin of base_is_value_
        ; struct# (which keys off a binding name); used at construction time in
        ; plan_binding# before the local is defined.
        function result_type_is_value_struct(this: Planner, t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked












        ; Hole-1 field provenance: true when `op` is a construction (a
        ; value-struct Tuple or an init# ctor Call) one of whose arguments is a
        ; `&<frame-local>` reference (result_type is pointer/ref with Local
        ; inner). Such an arg stores a frame address into one of the new
        ; struct's pointer fields. Scans Tuple component value-operands and
        ; Call args. Single-exit; only flat loops (no nested choose) to dodge
        ; the bootstrap-nest miscompile.
        function construction_has_local_ref_arg(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked






















































        ; Hole-1 field provenance: true when `op` reads a reference-like FIELD
        ; of a tainted frame-local value-struct binding (`h.p` where h was
        ; constructed with a &<frame-local> arg). Returning / storing / passing
        ; such a field hands out the frame address. Single-exit.
        function operand_reads_tainted_local_field(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked



















        ; taint_field_read_by_name#: at the member-access read site, if the base
        ; local binding `name` is TAINTED (constructed with a &<frame-local> arg)
        ; and the read FIELD type `t` is reference-like, return the same outer
        ; type with the inner pointee flipped to Local — the field holds a frame
        ; address, so the operand TYPE must carry that Local signal for the
        ; pass-to-callee post-pass (which runs after the per-fn taint set is torn
        ; down) to reject it via is_reference_to_local_page#. Identity (returns
        ; `t`) unless `name` is in the taint set, so a no-op for all non-test
        ; code (no tainted bindings there). Return/store are also covered by
        ; operand_reads_tainted_local_field#; this additionally makes the
        ; uniform check fire at the interprocedural call boundary.
        function taint_field_read_by_name(this: Planner, name: String, t: PlannedType) @page true @direct true @ret_param_2 true returns PlannedType linked








        ; ================================================================
        ; Escape checker (RBMM blocker C), Vector 3: pass-to-storing-callee.
        ;
        ; A reference into a Local ($) page passed to a callee that STORES
        ; it into frame-external memory (its `this`/param field, a global,
        ; or a pointer deref) is a use-after-free once the local page is
        ; freed at scope exit. Implemented as a POST-PASS Plan-tree walker
        ; (call discovery in ONE place rather than the many scattered
        ; PlannedCall construction sites in collapse_operand_sequence#).
        ;
        ; Two passes over every planned function body:
        ;   Pass 0 (collect): record which parameter a function stores
        ;                     frame-external, and into which destination.
        ;   Pass 1 (check):   at each call, if a local-page-ref arg is bound
        ;                     to such a param AND the destination outlives
        ;                     the arg -> hard diagnostic.
        ; Non-transitive (a param escaping only via a deeper escaping callee
        ; is not chased) — documented limitation, mirrors Planner.cpp.
        ;
        ; The walk mirrors the Emitter's body walk EXACTLY (emit_function_body
        ; -> emit_action -> emit_operands -> emit_operand / emit_statement),
        ; the only shape proven not to corrupt under stage-0 codegen.
        ; ================================================================

        ; True when `t` is a bare reference-like type (pointer[T]/ref[T]),
        ; regardless of pointee lifetime. Used at collect time on a callee's
        ; stored parameter; the local-page determination happens at the call
        ; site via is_reference_to_local_page. Mirrors isReferenceLikeType.
        function is_reference_like(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked










        ; True when a call's RESULT genuinely owns heap guts allocated on the
        ; passed page — i.e. the only return shape the default-to-local flip
        ; should stamp Local. Borrows/handles are EXCLUDED: pointer[T]/ref[T]
        ; (and ref[T]?/pointer[T]? under NPO, which resolve to name "ref"/
        ; "pointer") point into memory the callee already owns elsewhere, and
        ; extern LLVM-C wrapper structs (LLVMValueRef/TypeRef/MetadataRef/...)
        ; wrap LLVM-context-owned memory, not a Scaly page. Stamping any of
        ; these Local is a false escape (the flip's coarse `call_requires_page`
        ; gate fired on every class/union/wrapper return).
        function result_owns_page_guts(t: PlannedType) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked





























        ; Index of a parameter named `name` in the CURRENT function's Input
        ; (re-fetched via escape_fn_index), or -1. Excludes the page param
        ; (which is not part of the arg<->param mapping), matching the C++
        ; CalleeParamNames (Input only).
        function escape_param_index_current(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true returns int linked

























        ; The current function's Input length (the callee param count recorded
        ; in a summary entry; used to right-align a call's args).
        function escape_current_param_count(this: Planner) @ret_param_0 true returns size_t linked









        ; True when `name` is a parameter (or page receiver) of the CURRENT
        ; function — i.e. caller-provided memory that outlives this frame.
        ; Mirrors the EscapeFnParams membership test (Input + PageParam).
        function escape_name_is_current_param(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked































        ; A destination arg outlives the (local-page) arg passed alongside it
        ; when it refers to memory living beyond the current frame: a parameter
        ; of the current function, or a #/^ allocated value, or a module
        ; global. A same-frame $/stack local dies WITH the arg -> safe.
        ; Mirrors Planner.cpp::escapeDestArgOutlives.
        function escape_dest_arg_outlives(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked










































        ; binding_inits_stack_closure: true when a PlannedBinding's initializer
        ; is a stack (Lifetime.Local) closure — i.e. `let g: Cb \x: …` whose
        ; make_closure# stamped Local (env on the frame). Read structurally from
        ; the Plan tree so the escape post-pass needs no plan-time state.
        function binding_inits_stack_closure(this: Planner, binding: PlannedBinding) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked























        ; arg_is_stack_closure: true when a call ARGUMENT is a bare read (no
        ; member hop) of a name recorded in current_fn_closure_names — a stack
        ; closure of the calling function. Passing it to a callee that stores
        ; its parameter frame-external dangles once this frame exits.
        function arg_is_stack_closure(this: Planner, arg: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked















        ; escape_alias_param_index: the parameter index a local name aliases (via
        ; the current_fn_alias_names/params reshape map), or -1.
        function escape_alias_param_index(this: Planner, name: String) @ret_param_0 true returns int linked

















        ; EMITTER CONSTRAINT for every EscapeAccess-returning helper below:
        ; accumulate working state in SCALARS and construct the returned struct
        ; EXACTLY ONCE (a single tail construction). A by-value struct returned
        ; from multiple branches — or a struct local reassigned across branches —
        ; is region-allocated per site, and a phi merging those buffers fails
        ; LLVM verification ("Instruction does not dominate all uses"). Field
        ; paths are k=2, appended with truncation (a truncated path denotes "this
        ; field subtree" — region_of_arg_path# resolves it SAFE unless provably
        ; local, so truncation never causes a false flag).

        ; peel_operand: the inner operand of a deref (`*pr`) or a parenthesized
        ; single value (`(*pr)` = a one-component one-operand Tuple), as a ref
        ; into the existing Plan tree (no by-value union copy). null otherwise.
        ; Lets escape_resolve_access# keep a SINGLE recursion site outside its
        ; classifying choose — two sret-returning recursive calls in separate
        ; choose arms make the emitter merge their result buffers into a phi
        ; that fails LLVM verification ("does not dominate all uses").
        function peel_operand(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns ref[PlannedOperand]? linked








































        function escape_resolve_access(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true returns EscapeAccess linked





























































































        ; escape_value_param_access: the (parameter, field-path) an escapable
        ; stored/forwarded operand denotes, or param_index -1. A path-0 result is
        ; tracked ONLY when the operand is a bare parameter/alias Variable — the
        ; exact pre-reshape behavior of escape_value_param_root, so path-0 summary
        ; records are byte-identical. A bare deref/paren with no field (`(*pr)`)
        ; is not tracked. A field reshape (`(*pr).a`, `pr.a`, `s.current`) yields
        ; path_len > 0 and IS tracked (this is what escape_value_param_root
        ; deliberately dropped to protect the self-host; the field check now
        ; resolves it via per-field provenance instead of coarsely flagging).
        function escape_value_param_access(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true returns EscapeAccess linked




























        ; escape_record_field_points_to (Design-B 3e): seed the caller-side
        ; field-points-to map from a binding whose initializer is a VISIBLE
        ; struct construction (a Tuple). For each POSITIONAL component that is a
        ; reference and whose operand is LOCAL-region (region_of_operand below
        ; the escape threshold), record (bind_name, component_index -> field
        ; index, Local). Named components are skipped (positional index may not
        ; equal field index -> leave unrecorded -> Caller/safe). Only local
        ; fields are recorded; every other field resolves to Caller (safe) at
        ; region_of_arg_path#. Call-result / opaque initializers are not Tuples,
        ; so they are never seeded (sound: see the current_fn_fpt_* field
        ; comment). Runs in every pass; consulted only by the phase-4 check.
        procedure escape_record_field_points_to(mutable this: Planner, binding: PlannedBinding) linked





























































        ; escape_value_param_root: the reference-like/delegate PARAMETER index
        ; this operand's value IS — a bare parameter, or a bare local ALIAS of a
        ; parameter (`let q p`). -1 otherwise. Deliberately BARE only (no member
        ; hop). Kept UNCHANGED (not routed through escape_value_param_access) so
        ; the alias-recording site's bare-only semantics are exactly preserved.
        function escape_value_param_root(this: Planner, op: PlannedOperand) @ret_param_0 true returns int linked




















        ; Pass 0: at a frame-external store of a current-function parameter
        ; reference, record a summary entry. Reuses the Vector-2 store shape
        ; (value = a bare reference-like param; target = param.field / global
        ; / deref). Runs only in collect mode.
        procedure escape_handle_store(mutable this: Planner, act: PlannedAction) linked






























































































        ; fpt_region_of: the recorded region rank of local `base_name`'s field
        ; `field` in the current function's field-points-to map, or Caller
        ; (safe) when not recorded. Only LOCAL-region fields are ever recorded
        ; (escape_record_field_points_to#), so an unrecorded field is treated as
        ; Caller — the UNKNOWN -> SAFE default that keeps the self-host green.
        ; a record handed BY VALUE is copied into the callee's keep. Bound
        ; from a VISIBLE positional construction, its fields are what may
        ; point into this frame's local page; otherwise (a String from a
        ; call, an init) its Local stamp IS its guts — region_of_operand
        ; answers for references only. The addr-taken rule's promotion
        ; moved the binding onto the caller page.
        function deep_by_value_local(this: Planner, arg: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked







































        function lifetime_kind_name(lt: Lifetime) @page true @direct true @ret_caller true returns String linked

















        ; exactly the shapes restamp_putadd_constructions# moves onto the
        ; caller page: a construction, an init, a page-taking call, a tuple
        function operand_is_restampable(this: Planner, arg: PlannedOperand) @ret_param_1 true returns bool linked



















        ; does the field-points-to map hold a LOCAL field for the record the
        ; operand denotes (a bare `X` or `&X`)?
        function fpt_any_local(this: Planner, arg: PlannedOperand) @ret_nonlocal true returns bool linked

































































        function fpt_region_of(this: Planner, base_name: String, field: size_t) @ret_param_0 true returns size_t linked






















        ; region_of_arg_path: the region rank of the field the callee reaches
        ; into — arg.<src_field0> — for a FIELD-reshape record. The base local
        ; the arg denotes is a bare `X` or `&X`; its field region comes from the
        ; caller-side field-points-to map (fpt_region_of#). UNKNOWN -> Caller
        ; (SAFE): only a VISIBLY-locally-constructed struct's field resolves to
        ; Local, so a call-result / opaque / parameter arg is never flagged
        ; (this is what accepts save/restore-state and keeps the self-host
        ; green). Only depth-1 paths resolve precisely; a depth-2 (k-truncated)
        ; path -> Caller (safe), since the depth-1 map cannot prove the deeper
        ; field local without risking a false positive.
        function region_of_arg_path(this: Planner, arg: PlannedOperand, rec: EscapeRecord) @ret_nonlocal true returns size_t linked






















































        ; Pass 1: check one call against one summary entry.
        procedure escape_check_call_record(mutable this: Planner, call: PlannedCall, rec: EscapeRecord) linked




















































































        ; Pass 1 (indirect calls): an indirect call goes THROUGH a delegate
        ; VALUE whose body is unknown at compile time, so we cannot prove it
        ; does not stash its arguments. Conservatively (SOUND) reject passing
        ; any escaping argument to it — a reference into a local page, or a
        ; stack closure of this frame. args[0] is the callee (the delegate
        ; value itself); the real arguments are args[1..]. A borrowed parameter
        ; or a '#'/'^' allocation is Caller-ranked and stays fine. This closes
        ; the non-transitivity hole for a chain whose escaping hop is an
        ; unresolved (function-pointer) call — the direct-call summary path
        ; keys on mangled_name, which an indirect call does not have.
        procedure escape_check_indirect_call(mutable this: Planner, call: PlannedCall) linked









































        ; Pass 1: a call may match several summary entries (e.g. two escaping
        ; params); check each.
        ; ====================================================================
        ; ★★★A REFERENCE PARAMETER PUT INTO A PAGE-HOSTED CONSTRUCTION ESCAPES
        ; WITH THAT OBJECT (2026-09-20). The checker refuses a frame-local
        ; reference that is RETURNED or stored by a plain `set` -- both
        ; measured loud -- and said NOTHING when the reference travelled into a
        ; callee that puts it inside a NEW HEAP OBJECT. In packages/tscaly 174
        ; frame-local lists reached `box_node_data[T](host, T(... list ...))`
        ; and compiled clean; the program died of an UNBOUNDED RECURSION (a
        ; dangling list feeds a memo, the memo answers nonsense, the cycle
        ; guard never matches) that the whole gate chain missed -- only a run
        ; over a 4 000-file corpus reached it.
        ;
        ; ★★★It is RECORDED in the summary, not diagnosed here, and that is the
        ; design: with the callee marked, the call-site machinery that already
        ; refuses "a frame-local reference passed to a storing callee" fires by
        ; itself, with its own message and its own line -- and it fires ONLY
        ; where the argument really is frame-local, which is why marking every
        ; factory that boxes a reference costs nothing.
        ;
        ; ★A PAGE-HOSTED construction is the condition (`is_region_alloc`:
        ; `T^name(...)`, `T#(...)`), because such an object outlives the
        ; callee's frame by construction. A plain value construction is not:
        ; `Slice[T](n, p)` borrows for the caller's own frame, and flagging it
        ; would refuse every `s.subslice(a, b)` over a local buffer.
        ;
        ; ★It records during the SUMMARY passes only (`escape_checking` false),
        ; like every other recorder here, and shares their dedup.
        procedure escape_record_construction_param(mutable this: Planner, tp: PlannedTuple) io linked





















































        ; The record itself: destination kind 1 is "frame-external" (what a
        ; global or a deref store uses), which is exactly what a page-hosted
        ; object is from the callee's point of view.
        procedure escape_record_param_into_region(mutable this: Planner, v: PlannedOperand) io linked































        procedure escape_handle_call(mutable this: Planner, call: PlannedCall) io linked



































        ; True when the summary already carries a record equal to this one
        ; (callee_fn_index + param_index + dest_kind + dest_index). callee_param_
